1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,970
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:01,970 --> 00:00:03,930
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:03,930 --> 00:00:07,360
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:12,848 --> 00:00:14,140
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Hi, everyone.

5
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:15,520
So today, I'm actually just going

6
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:18,600
to tell you about some of my research, which

7
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:20,320
is about deep learning.

8
00:00:20,320 --> 00:00:26,580
And again, you can take everything out--

9
00:00:26,580 --> 00:00:29,980
you don't have to fully trust me in this lecture.

10
00:00:29,980 --> 00:00:31,860
And maybe you should be skeptical.

11
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:43,200
So the idea that I want to talk about is the idea of the way

12
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:46,280
that we build neural networks is kind of like a LEGO set,

13
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:48,060
where we have all these little pieces,

14
00:00:48,060 --> 00:00:51,440
all these building blocks, and we piece them together,

15
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:56,377
and we can build a transformer, for example.

16
00:00:56,377 --> 00:00:57,960
But we could take those pieces, and we

17
00:00:57,960 --> 00:00:59,310
could build something else.

18
00:00:59,310 --> 00:01:03,790
There's definitely this modular structure.

19
00:01:03,790 --> 00:01:07,670
So if that's how we build neural networks,

20
00:01:07,670 --> 00:01:10,350
why don't we also build our theoretical understanding

21
00:01:10,350 --> 00:01:15,190
of them in exactly the same way?

22
00:01:15,190 --> 00:01:18,790
And arguably, if you want to have a theoretical understanding

23
00:01:18,790 --> 00:01:21,910
of deep learning that really applies to any neural network

24
00:01:21,910 --> 00:01:23,990
that you actually find in the wild,

25
00:01:23,990 --> 00:01:28,113
you better build the theory in the same way

26
00:01:28,113 --> 00:01:29,530
that you build the neural network.

27
00:01:29,530 --> 00:01:31,110
Otherwise, how can it possibly hope

28
00:01:31,110 --> 00:01:33,310
to describe actual neural networks

29
00:01:33,310 --> 00:01:37,430
that you find out in the wild?

30
00:01:37,430 --> 00:01:41,150
So what does that mean to build the theory in the same way

31
00:01:41,150 --> 00:01:42,730
as you build the neural network?

32
00:01:45,630 --> 00:01:49,830
So the idea is if you think about an individual piece

33
00:01:49,830 --> 00:01:52,910
of LEGO, imagine if you had a full theoretical

34
00:01:52,910 --> 00:01:56,070
characterization of that single object, which

35
00:01:56,070 --> 00:01:59,920
is maybe reasonable because it's a simple little piece.

36
00:02:02,900 --> 00:02:06,420
Then, if we think about what are the operations that we have

37
00:02:06,420 --> 00:02:09,900
for combining little pieces of LEGO

38
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:14,140
to build more complicated structures, and the two

39
00:02:14,140 --> 00:02:17,820
that we're going to think about is what we call a series

40
00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:20,180
combination, or putting one on top of the other,

41
00:02:20,180 --> 00:02:26,000
or a parallel combination, which is putting them side by side.

42
00:02:30,660 --> 00:02:34,820
And then we should ask, what properties of an individual LEGO

43
00:02:34,820 --> 00:02:37,940
piece do we actually care about, or of an individual layer

44
00:02:37,940 --> 00:02:38,840
in a neural network?

45
00:02:38,840 --> 00:02:39,920
What do we care about?

46
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:46,780
So a layer in a neural network has an input.

47
00:02:46,780 --> 00:02:50,200
It has weights and an output.

48
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:57,870
And the question that we're going to ask is how--

49
00:02:57,870 --> 00:03:00,120
the thing that we're really going to care about is how

50
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:04,160
sensitive is the output of the layer in this case to both

51
00:03:04,160 --> 00:03:06,120
changing the input-- so you think about adding

52
00:03:06,120 --> 00:03:09,200
a perturbation to the input--

53
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:13,120
or what if we perturb the weights?

54
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:17,080
The question is, how much does the output change?

55
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:19,880
And think that the change in output

56
00:03:19,880 --> 00:03:23,605
gets contributions both from the change in the inputs

57
00:03:23,605 --> 00:03:25,480
and the change in the weights and potentially

58
00:03:25,480 --> 00:03:27,500
the interactions of those two things.

59
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:33,900
And what I want to argue is that, first of all,

60
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:35,180
no one understands this.

61
00:03:35,180 --> 00:03:37,360
It's a really basic question about neural networks,

62
00:03:37,360 --> 00:03:40,220
but no one teaches you about it.

63
00:03:40,220 --> 00:03:42,400
No one understands it.

64
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:45,680
But it's a really important question

65
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:47,260
for a variety of different reasons.

66
00:03:47,260 --> 00:03:50,280
But one of them is optimization because, if you think about it,

67
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:52,720
training a neural network involves changing the weights

68
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:57,140
and changing the weights of all the different layers.

69
00:03:57,140 --> 00:03:59,390
And another reason you should care about this question

70
00:03:59,390 --> 00:04:01,890
is things like robustness.

71
00:04:01,890 --> 00:04:04,110
People talk about adversarial examples,

72
00:04:04,110 --> 00:04:06,550
where you change the input to the network and the output

73
00:04:06,550 --> 00:04:08,170
changes dramatically.

74
00:04:08,170 --> 00:04:10,310
So it's another question of understanding

75
00:04:10,310 --> 00:04:12,170
the sensitivity of the network.

76
00:04:18,630 --> 00:04:20,968
And so the high-level way that I want

77
00:04:20,968 --> 00:04:22,510
to think about this question is let's

78
00:04:22,510 --> 00:04:24,550
say that we can characterize that information

79
00:04:24,550 --> 00:04:28,350
for an individual piece of the network,

80
00:04:28,350 --> 00:04:31,910
can we extend this understanding to combinations?

81
00:04:31,910 --> 00:04:36,710
So if we put the bricks or we put the layers into a series

82
00:04:36,710 --> 00:04:39,830
combination, that's a little more complicated

83
00:04:39,830 --> 00:04:42,390
because now there's two.

84
00:04:42,390 --> 00:04:45,455
Each piece has its own weight space.

85
00:04:45,455 --> 00:04:46,830
If we combine them, we're somehow

86
00:04:46,830 --> 00:04:50,430
combining the weight space.

87
00:04:50,430 --> 00:04:55,540
And similarly for putting them in parallel,

88
00:04:55,540 --> 00:04:59,380
we get an object with a combined weight space.

89
00:04:59,380 --> 00:05:02,800
And now there's-- you think of this as duplicating the input,

90
00:05:02,800 --> 00:05:06,080
sending it to both of the pieces,

91
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:09,740
and then we get two outputs now.

92
00:05:09,740 --> 00:05:12,900
And if you think about it, somehow the series combination

93
00:05:12,900 --> 00:05:15,880
is somehow a bit more coupled than the parallel combination,

94
00:05:15,880 --> 00:05:20,540
but we've still got to think about both of these things.

95
00:05:20,540 --> 00:05:22,960
And then if we can really do this,

96
00:05:22,960 --> 00:05:27,500
if we can really understand the properties of individual pieces

97
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:29,700
and then how those properties get transformed

98
00:05:29,700 --> 00:05:32,500
through combinations, then we can really

99
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:35,620
think of a deep learning library, something

100
00:05:35,620 --> 00:05:38,540
like PyTorch or Jax.

101
00:05:38,540 --> 00:05:42,140
You should really think of it like a LEGO set,

102
00:05:42,140 --> 00:05:43,940
where you've got all these layers,

103
00:05:43,940 --> 00:05:48,060
and each layer should have its own theoretical description.

104
00:05:48,060 --> 00:05:51,370
And then we should have a system of rules

105
00:05:51,370 --> 00:05:54,810
for if you combine layers, the new object that you get

106
00:05:54,810 --> 00:05:56,150
has these properties.

107
00:05:58,770 --> 00:06:00,790
And then once you've got those things,

108
00:06:00,790 --> 00:06:02,810
you should be able to build whatever you want.

109
00:06:02,810 --> 00:06:06,250
You can go and take this library and build whatever

110
00:06:06,250 --> 00:06:09,265
fanciful creation you like.

111
00:06:09,265 --> 00:06:10,390
And that should be allowed.

112
00:06:10,390 --> 00:06:12,348
And you should have a theoretical understanding

113
00:06:12,348 --> 00:06:15,850
of that thing automatically.

114
00:06:15,850 --> 00:06:20,690
So that's how we're thinking about deep learning.

115
00:06:20,690 --> 00:06:25,270
And there's actually a practical payoff already in certain axes.

116
00:06:25,270 --> 00:06:27,810
So I just want to tell you a little bit about that

117
00:06:27,810 --> 00:06:30,530
because otherwise you wouldn't know whether or not

118
00:06:30,530 --> 00:06:33,170
you should bother listening.

119
00:06:33,170 --> 00:06:37,890
So what I want to claim is that if you adopt this perspective,

120
00:06:37,890 --> 00:06:40,330
it provides an easy way to fix these scaling

121
00:06:40,330 --> 00:06:42,050
issues that people have.

122
00:06:42,050 --> 00:06:46,490
So we talked a bit about this in one of the earlier lectures

123
00:06:46,490 --> 00:06:47,110
in the class.

124
00:06:47,110 --> 00:06:48,568
But you have these issues where you

125
00:06:48,568 --> 00:06:52,070
scale the width of building blocks in the network

126
00:06:52,070 --> 00:06:57,670
and the whole optimization landscape kind of drifts,

127
00:06:57,670 --> 00:07:02,110
even though the performance is getting better,

128
00:07:02,110 --> 00:07:04,310
the minima in the optimization landscape

129
00:07:04,310 --> 00:07:08,010
is shifting to a different optimal learning rate.

130
00:07:10,550 --> 00:07:12,430
And then, actually, people have recently

131
00:07:12,430 --> 00:07:14,910
taken some of these ideas and packaged them

132
00:07:14,910 --> 00:07:20,550
with some other ideas but got much faster training.

133
00:07:20,550 --> 00:07:22,950
So this also relates to the homework problem

134
00:07:22,950 --> 00:07:26,170
about the steepest descent algorithms.

135
00:07:26,170 --> 00:07:28,770
But this is all in the last couple of months,

136
00:07:28,770 --> 00:07:33,590
but this is showing a comparison of training speed.

137
00:07:33,590 --> 00:07:37,710
And the baseline is llm.c, which is like Andrej Karpathy's

138
00:07:37,710 --> 00:07:41,710
extremely high-performance C implementation,

139
00:07:41,710 --> 00:07:44,830
but with some of these ideas about steepest descent

140
00:07:44,830 --> 00:07:49,160
algorithms based on norms and spectral descent-type things,

141
00:07:49,160 --> 00:07:51,080
they actually got way faster training.

142
00:07:51,080 --> 00:07:53,100
So this is a huge wall clock speedup

143
00:07:53,100 --> 00:07:57,400
in training by integrating some of these ideas.

144
00:07:57,400 --> 00:07:59,100
So this is just to give you a sense

145
00:07:59,100 --> 00:08:01,600
that actually maybe some of this stuff matters.

146
00:08:09,480 --> 00:08:13,580
So far, I've tried to put across this-- oh yeah, go ahead.

147
00:08:13,580 --> 00:08:16,380
AUDIENCE: In this method, you showed that it was much faster

148
00:08:16,380 --> 00:08:17,620
than the other one.

149
00:08:17,620 --> 00:08:20,720
Do they know why it's so much faster,

150
00:08:20,720 --> 00:08:23,800
or just they found that this is faster, but not really sure why?

151
00:08:26,500 --> 00:08:28,520
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Partly understand why.

152
00:08:28,520 --> 00:08:30,340
Yeah, we partly understand why.

153
00:08:30,340 --> 00:08:34,020
Basically, the difference between the faster curves

154
00:08:34,020 --> 00:08:36,000
are using this spectral descent algorithm,

155
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,940
which uses steepest descent under the spectral norm, which

156
00:08:38,940 --> 00:08:40,200
was in the homework problem.

157
00:08:40,200 --> 00:08:42,179
And if you believe that the geometry

158
00:08:42,179 --> 00:08:45,450
of an individual linear layer is somehow

159
00:08:45,450 --> 00:08:49,450
described by the spectral norm, then that would explain why.

160
00:08:49,450 --> 00:08:51,670
So it's just integrating that idea.

161
00:08:58,170 --> 00:09:00,430
But we'll go a little more into things.

162
00:09:00,430 --> 00:09:02,410
But I first want to zoom out.

163
00:09:02,410 --> 00:09:04,850
And so we talked about optimization theory earlier

164
00:09:04,850 --> 00:09:08,330
in the class, but I want to try to connect optimization

165
00:09:08,330 --> 00:09:12,230
theory to this idea of building neural networks like a LEGO set.

166
00:09:15,330 --> 00:09:20,090
So if you remember, let's say that we have our loss function,

167
00:09:20,090 --> 00:09:22,150
which is like this L symbol.

168
00:09:22,150 --> 00:09:25,170
And we've got an n-dimensional parameter space.

169
00:09:25,170 --> 00:09:27,610
And then the loss function maps the set of parameters

170
00:09:27,610 --> 00:09:29,250
to a real number.

171
00:09:29,250 --> 00:09:32,890
And let's say that we can Taylor expand the loss.

172
00:09:32,890 --> 00:09:35,730
So if you remember, there's the first-order term,

173
00:09:35,730 --> 00:09:39,090
and then there's the Hessian term,

174
00:09:39,090 --> 00:09:42,560
and then there's higher-order terms in the Taylor expansion.

175
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:44,880
And if you remember, what we said is,

176
00:09:44,880 --> 00:09:49,320
what if we could come up with a norm and a sharpness parameter

177
00:09:49,320 --> 00:09:52,827
lambda such that we can upper bound-- at least

178
00:09:52,827 --> 00:09:55,160
we can upper bound that second order piece in the Taylor

179
00:09:55,160 --> 00:09:56,700
expansion using this norm?

180
00:09:59,600 --> 00:10:03,820
So yeah, we were saying, what if we could do this?

181
00:10:03,820 --> 00:10:05,320
What would it mean?

182
00:10:05,320 --> 00:10:09,040
And in particular, can we find a norm and a sharpness parameter

183
00:10:09,040 --> 00:10:10,940
such that this is a really tight upper bound?

184
00:10:10,940 --> 00:10:13,440
That would also be really great because then we can actually

185
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:16,360
think of using this bound as an actual description of the loss

186
00:10:16,360 --> 00:10:19,560
function.

187
00:10:19,560 --> 00:10:21,800
Well, if we could do that, what we said

188
00:10:21,800 --> 00:10:24,480
and what we spent time on the homework thinking about

189
00:10:24,480 --> 00:10:29,900
is this thing called steepest descent optimization algorithms,

190
00:10:29,900 --> 00:10:34,440
using particular norms that we can think about picking

191
00:10:34,440 --> 00:10:38,520
an update step to minimize this, the linearization of the loss,

192
00:10:38,520 --> 00:10:41,780
plus the penalty term that involves the norm.

193
00:10:41,780 --> 00:10:46,600
And then we can think about if I pick this norm,

194
00:10:46,600 --> 00:10:48,047
I get this optimization algorithm.

195
00:10:48,047 --> 00:10:50,380
If I pick this other norm, I get this other optimization

196
00:10:50,380 --> 00:10:53,660
algorithm.

197
00:10:53,660 --> 00:10:57,740
So how should we actually produce such a norm?

198
00:10:57,740 --> 00:10:59,880
What is a good norm for a neural network?

199
00:10:59,880 --> 00:11:02,420
Why would we favor one norm over a different norm?

200
00:11:02,420 --> 00:11:05,660
I think that's an important question.

201
00:11:05,660 --> 00:11:07,660
So that's what we're going to try to think about

202
00:11:07,660 --> 00:11:10,200
over the next few slides.

203
00:11:13,900 --> 00:11:18,220
And just remember that-- so we're trying to produce a norm

204
00:11:18,220 --> 00:11:21,707
and a sharpness parameter such that the second-order piece

205
00:11:21,707 --> 00:11:23,540
in the Taylor expansion of our loss function

206
00:11:23,540 --> 00:11:28,620
is upper bounded by that object in the red box.

207
00:11:28,620 --> 00:11:30,700
So how are we going to think about this?

208
00:11:30,700 --> 00:11:33,660
Well, first of all, remember that, in deep learning, we have

209
00:11:33,660 --> 00:11:35,820
what's called a composite loss function.

210
00:11:35,820 --> 00:11:38,690
So the total loss is the error measure composed

211
00:11:38,690 --> 00:11:40,230
with the neural network.

212
00:11:40,230 --> 00:11:44,010
So that the error measure is l and the neural network is f.

213
00:11:44,010 --> 00:11:45,883
So it's a composite structure.

214
00:11:45,883 --> 00:11:48,050
And then also remember that we have something called

215
00:11:48,050 --> 00:11:49,550
the Gauss-Newton decomposition.

216
00:11:49,550 --> 00:11:53,610
So whenever you have a composite loss function where

217
00:11:53,610 --> 00:11:58,130
both parts of it are suitably differentiable,

218
00:11:58,130 --> 00:12:01,810
we can decompose the second-order term in the loss

219
00:12:01,810 --> 00:12:03,570
function into two pieces.

220
00:12:03,570 --> 00:12:07,590
So the first one involves the curvature of the neural network,

221
00:12:07,590 --> 00:12:09,650
and the second piece involves the curvature

222
00:12:09,650 --> 00:12:11,930
of the error measure.

223
00:12:11,930 --> 00:12:14,570
So that step one is just like, because we have a composite,

224
00:12:14,570 --> 00:12:18,850
we have the Gauss-Newton decomposition.

225
00:12:18,850 --> 00:12:24,890
So step two is now let's suppose that we know a really good norm

226
00:12:24,890 --> 00:12:26,890
on the output of the network.

227
00:12:26,890 --> 00:12:28,852
And maybe that's reasonable because the output

228
00:12:28,852 --> 00:12:30,310
of the network is often something--

229
00:12:30,310 --> 00:12:32,030
it's a simple thing like a vector,

230
00:12:32,030 --> 00:12:34,570
like either the network tends to output a vector

231
00:12:34,570 --> 00:12:36,190
or it outputs a real number.

232
00:12:36,190 --> 00:12:38,690
So we can usually come up with a good norm.

233
00:12:38,690 --> 00:12:40,440
AUDIENCE: So what would it mean for a norm

234
00:12:40,440 --> 00:12:42,380
to be bad in this instance?

235
00:12:46,632 --> 00:12:48,340
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: That's a good question.

236
00:12:51,520 --> 00:12:55,040
Well, at least if you just have the output of the network,

237
00:12:55,040 --> 00:12:57,240
and it's a vector with the kind of there's

238
00:12:57,240 --> 00:12:59,640
no obvious extra structure on that thing other

239
00:12:59,640 --> 00:13:03,280
than it's a vector, there's only a few norms you would really

240
00:13:03,280 --> 00:13:05,640
think of, Lp norms, basically.

241
00:13:05,640 --> 00:13:07,660
But if you have a more complicated object,

242
00:13:07,660 --> 00:13:10,040
like a full neural network, there's a whole zoo of norms

243
00:13:10,040 --> 00:13:11,620
that you might think of.

244
00:13:11,620 --> 00:13:14,520
So it's at least like maybe you give the output

245
00:13:14,520 --> 00:13:20,600
the infinity norm, the L2 norm, the RMS norm, the L1 norm.

246
00:13:20,600 --> 00:13:22,780
There's not many other obvious choices.

247
00:13:26,680 --> 00:13:29,380
So that's what I mean.

248
00:13:29,380 --> 00:13:31,580
And again, I may be wrong about these things,

249
00:13:31,580 --> 00:13:36,750
so be a little skeptical because this is just the way that we've

250
00:13:36,750 --> 00:13:40,550
started thinking about it.

251
00:13:40,550 --> 00:13:45,550
So the point is that if we have a norm on the output,

252
00:13:45,550 --> 00:13:47,950
we can take the Gauss-Newton decomposition,

253
00:13:47,950 --> 00:13:56,670
and then we can actually bound the two terms.

254
00:13:56,670 --> 00:14:00,630
And the reason that we can do that

255
00:14:00,630 --> 00:14:09,670
is essentially because you can read the term in orange

256
00:14:09,670 --> 00:14:13,070
as being a dot product between the curvature of the model

257
00:14:13,070 --> 00:14:16,350
and the linearization of the error measure.

258
00:14:16,350 --> 00:14:18,710
And then you can apply the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality

259
00:14:18,710 --> 00:14:21,350
to that term.

260
00:14:21,350 --> 00:14:24,790
So the norm on the derivative of the error measure

261
00:14:24,790 --> 00:14:26,470
is actually a dual norm corresponding

262
00:14:26,470 --> 00:14:29,630
to the dual norm of the norm and network outputs.

263
00:14:29,630 --> 00:14:34,090
And similarly, you can think about the purple term

264
00:14:34,090 --> 00:14:39,470
as being like a vector times a matrix times a vector,

265
00:14:39,470 --> 00:14:42,090
and there's an operator norm upper bound on that thing.

266
00:14:42,090 --> 00:14:46,810
So basically, anyway, long story short,

267
00:14:46,810 --> 00:14:50,110
you can bound these terms if you have a norm on a network output.

268
00:14:54,690 --> 00:14:57,433
So that's step two is just observing

269
00:14:57,433 --> 00:14:58,850
that you can upper bound the terms

270
00:14:58,850 --> 00:15:00,430
in the Gauss-Newton decomposition,

271
00:15:00,430 --> 00:15:05,610
so no approximations, just an upper bound.

272
00:15:05,610 --> 00:15:10,090
So step three is just restating where we got up to.

273
00:15:10,090 --> 00:15:13,430
We've upper bounded the terms in the Gauss-Newton decomposition.

274
00:15:13,430 --> 00:15:15,970
And then we can now actually-- remember,

275
00:15:15,970 --> 00:15:21,090
our original problem was finding this red box

276
00:15:21,090 --> 00:15:24,090
finding this upper bound on the second-order piece of the loss

277
00:15:24,090 --> 00:15:25,070
function.

278
00:15:25,070 --> 00:15:28,890
But we've actually reduced our problem because now

279
00:15:28,890 --> 00:15:36,320
if we can upper bound the first term in orange

280
00:15:36,320 --> 00:15:38,338
with a norm using a norm on the weights,

281
00:15:38,338 --> 00:15:40,880
so if we can produce a norm on the weights which upper bounds

282
00:15:40,880 --> 00:15:47,360
that first orange term and if we can produce a norm on the weight

283
00:15:47,360 --> 00:15:55,600
space to upper bound this last term in purple,

284
00:15:55,600 --> 00:16:00,240
if we can do that, you can plug those

285
00:16:00,240 --> 00:16:02,860
into the upper bound on the Gauss-Newton decomposition.

286
00:16:02,860 --> 00:16:04,960
It will give us an upper bound on the curvature

287
00:16:04,960 --> 00:16:06,342
of the overall loss function.

288
00:16:06,342 --> 00:16:08,800
So we've kind of reduced the problem which we didn't really

289
00:16:08,800 --> 00:16:12,580
know how to solve now into at least a different problem,

290
00:16:12,580 --> 00:16:15,800
and then the question is, can we solve that other problem?

291
00:16:15,800 --> 00:16:20,160
And then I've just written beneath these two expressions

292
00:16:20,160 --> 00:16:23,000
that the first one is asking that the network is Lipschitz

293
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:26,160
smooth in its weights, and the second one

294
00:16:26,160 --> 00:16:28,040
is asking that the network is Lipschitz

295
00:16:28,040 --> 00:16:31,590
continuous in its weights.

296
00:16:31,590 --> 00:16:36,070
I'll just pause briefly.

297
00:16:36,070 --> 00:16:38,230
So it's just to say that we started out

298
00:16:38,230 --> 00:16:40,933
with a problem about the overall loss function.

299
00:16:40,933 --> 00:16:42,350
We upper bounded this thing called

300
00:16:42,350 --> 00:16:44,830
the Gauss-Newton decomposition.

301
00:16:44,830 --> 00:16:46,830
And then we related the terms in that

302
00:16:46,830 --> 00:16:48,490
to properties of the network.

303
00:16:53,290 --> 00:16:55,430
This is supposed to be a more pictorial or somehow

304
00:16:55,430 --> 00:16:58,350
visual description of what's going on.

305
00:16:58,350 --> 00:17:01,150
So we said that we reduced to wanting

306
00:17:01,150 --> 00:17:04,109
to have these conditions on our neural network, so

307
00:17:04,109 --> 00:17:09,030
a norm on the weight space such that the network is Lipschitz

308
00:17:09,030 --> 00:17:12,589
smooth and Lipschitz continuous in that norm.

309
00:17:12,589 --> 00:17:15,670
AUDIENCE: So if it's Lipschitz smooth and Lipschitz continuous,

310
00:17:15,670 --> 00:17:22,030
that guarantees existence of such alphas and deltas?

311
00:17:22,030 --> 00:17:23,990
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes, but--

312
00:17:23,990 --> 00:17:28,060
AUDIENCE: For any norm or [INAUDIBLE]?

313
00:17:28,060 --> 00:17:30,740
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Well, you could take these actually

314
00:17:30,740 --> 00:17:34,240
as the definitions of Lipschitz smooth and Lipschitz continuous

315
00:17:34,240 --> 00:17:36,340
and once you have them in one norm,

316
00:17:36,340 --> 00:17:38,740
you can automatically produce them in any other norm

317
00:17:38,740 --> 00:17:40,720
because all the norms are equivalent

318
00:17:40,720 --> 00:17:44,220
but it may be a much tighter guarantee

319
00:17:44,220 --> 00:17:46,140
in 1 norm than another norm.

320
00:17:46,140 --> 00:17:48,940
So we really want to find a norm on the weight space

321
00:17:48,940 --> 00:17:55,020
such that these conditions hold very, very tightly.

322
00:17:55,020 --> 00:17:57,360
But just to describe pictorially what's going on,

323
00:17:57,360 --> 00:18:00,340
so we've got a neural network, and we

324
00:18:00,340 --> 00:18:04,540
think about passing an input into it, and it has weights

325
00:18:04,540 --> 00:18:06,148
and it has an output.

326
00:18:06,148 --> 00:18:07,940
And what you can think of doing is actually

327
00:18:07,940 --> 00:18:12,538
Taylor expanding the neural network in its weights.

328
00:18:12,538 --> 00:18:14,580
So remember, previously, we were Taylor expanding

329
00:18:14,580 --> 00:18:16,380
the loss in the weights, but now we've

330
00:18:16,380 --> 00:18:18,780
mapped things to actually just thinking directly

331
00:18:18,780 --> 00:18:21,340
about the network.

332
00:18:21,340 --> 00:18:24,060
And these Lipschitz conditions are

333
00:18:24,060 --> 00:18:29,200
asking for upper bounds on the terms in the Taylor expansion

334
00:18:29,200 --> 00:18:31,040
in a particular norm.

335
00:18:31,040 --> 00:18:33,600
So can we produce a norm on the weight space

336
00:18:33,600 --> 00:18:37,840
such that we get nice, convenient upper bounds

337
00:18:37,840 --> 00:18:39,160
on these terms?

338
00:18:39,160 --> 00:18:41,580
And so forgetting everything that we talked about,

339
00:18:41,580 --> 00:18:44,280
about optimization, just directly looking at this slide

340
00:18:44,280 --> 00:18:46,260
and just directly thinking about it,

341
00:18:46,260 --> 00:18:49,200
wouldn't it be great if we had a norm on the weights that

342
00:18:49,200 --> 00:18:51,840
could predict the magnitude of these terms in the Taylor

343
00:18:51,840 --> 00:18:52,680
expansion?

344
00:18:52,680 --> 00:18:54,760
If we had such a norm, it would mean

345
00:18:54,760 --> 00:18:56,762
that whenever you were going to change

346
00:18:56,762 --> 00:18:58,220
the weights of your neural network,

347
00:18:58,220 --> 00:19:00,360
you would have a direct way to predict

348
00:19:00,360 --> 00:19:02,000
how much the output of the network

349
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,700
can change up to first and second order.

350
00:19:04,700 --> 00:19:07,400
So there's just a direct argument

351
00:19:07,400 --> 00:19:09,760
that having such a norm would be a really useful thing

352
00:19:09,760 --> 00:19:12,240
to have because we can now predict what our network is

353
00:19:12,240 --> 00:19:14,560
going to do in response to changing the weights.

354
00:19:14,560 --> 00:19:17,680
And then all of the preceding argument was saying,

355
00:19:17,680 --> 00:19:21,480
one of the use cases for having these relationships

356
00:19:21,480 --> 00:19:25,470
is that we can build an optimization theory around them,

357
00:19:25,470 --> 00:19:27,590
but they can be useful for other reasons as well.

358
00:19:27,590 --> 00:19:30,602
They're useful anytime you want to be able to just predict

359
00:19:30,602 --> 00:19:32,310
what changing the weights of your network

360
00:19:32,310 --> 00:19:34,250
is going to do to the overall function.

361
00:19:44,550 --> 00:19:48,710
So what we've tried to argue is that having these conditions

362
00:19:48,710 --> 00:19:51,550
would be useful, and it would help us build an optimization

363
00:19:51,550 --> 00:19:54,230
theory, but it seems like, actually, how on Earth

364
00:19:54,230 --> 00:19:57,830
would we produce a norm, which is a really good norm

365
00:19:57,830 --> 00:20:01,510
for this reason because it seems that neural networks are really

366
00:20:01,510 --> 00:20:03,770
complicated, so how are we going to do that?

367
00:20:03,770 --> 00:20:05,470
It seems really hard.

368
00:20:05,470 --> 00:20:06,118
Go ahead.

369
00:20:06,118 --> 00:20:08,410
AUDIENCE: I don't even know if my question makes sense,

370
00:20:08,410 --> 00:20:12,110
but I would like to know if people have used this approach

371
00:20:12,110 --> 00:20:15,743
to find approximation for other types of problems,

372
00:20:15,743 --> 00:20:17,910
so say [INAUDIBLE] neural network or something else,

373
00:20:17,910 --> 00:20:19,368
you didn't know how to approximate,

374
00:20:19,368 --> 00:20:21,300
and then you found [INAUDIBLE] and has

375
00:20:21,300 --> 00:20:22,598
this worked in [INAUDIBLE].

376
00:20:22,598 --> 00:20:24,140
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, what I'll say

377
00:20:24,140 --> 00:20:26,920
is that a lot of classical optimization,

378
00:20:26,920 --> 00:20:31,340
once you start to get into it, is all about finding a distance

379
00:20:31,340 --> 00:20:32,980
measure for your problem.

380
00:20:32,980 --> 00:20:35,280
And once you've got a really good distance measure,

381
00:20:35,280 --> 00:20:38,320
you can start to design clever algorithms.

382
00:20:41,740 --> 00:20:44,540
And people have that idea in deep learning

383
00:20:44,540 --> 00:20:45,820
but haven't really--

384
00:20:45,820 --> 00:20:48,220
it's not really borne fruit, I would say.

385
00:20:48,220 --> 00:20:52,400
But it is a very classical idea to do this general thing.

386
00:20:52,400 --> 00:20:56,060
And then there is you can look up composite optimization

387
00:20:56,060 --> 00:20:58,440
theory, and there's lots of papers about it.

388
00:20:58,440 --> 00:21:03,140
But I would say that it's not really borne

389
00:21:03,140 --> 00:21:04,600
fruit in the deep learning.

390
00:21:04,600 --> 00:21:09,440
But it's definitely that idea is very abundant, I would say.

391
00:21:14,743 --> 00:21:17,160
If you think about it, that's why we have different norms.

392
00:21:17,160 --> 00:21:20,470
Why do we have the L1 norm and L2 norm?

393
00:21:20,470 --> 00:21:21,930
Because somehow, they're supposed

394
00:21:21,930 --> 00:21:25,690
to characterize the structure of different problems.

395
00:21:25,690 --> 00:21:28,590
If they didn't, we would just only have one of them.

396
00:21:28,590 --> 00:21:30,610
So that's like why there's a whole zoo of norms

397
00:21:30,610 --> 00:21:34,270
is to somehow use them to characterize different problems.

398
00:21:40,490 --> 00:21:43,850
So this is where the whole LEGO thing comes

399
00:21:43,850 --> 00:21:46,290
back again because it's like, how on Earth

400
00:21:46,290 --> 00:21:48,530
are we going to come up with a norm on the weight

401
00:21:48,530 --> 00:21:50,250
space of our whole network?

402
00:21:50,250 --> 00:21:53,590
Well, what if we can break it up into stages?

403
00:21:53,590 --> 00:21:57,650
So if we first think about the activation spaces,

404
00:21:57,650 --> 00:21:59,330
again, those are more like simple

405
00:21:59,330 --> 00:22:01,350
just vectors in a simple network.

406
00:22:01,350 --> 00:22:05,250
So as we said, it's kind of easier

407
00:22:05,250 --> 00:22:07,090
to give those norms because there's not

408
00:22:07,090 --> 00:22:10,830
that many options, like L2, L infinity, L1, RMS.

409
00:22:13,530 --> 00:22:16,730
Now let's think about the tensor spaces, like the matrix spaces,

410
00:22:16,730 --> 00:22:18,410
for example, in the network.

411
00:22:18,410 --> 00:22:21,190
Once we've picked norms on the activation spaces,

412
00:22:21,190 --> 00:22:24,550
there's actually a way to induce something called

413
00:22:24,550 --> 00:22:26,510
an induced operator norm.

414
00:22:26,510 --> 00:22:30,110
But there's a procedure for taking two norms on the input

415
00:22:30,110 --> 00:22:32,190
and output of a linear operator and coming up

416
00:22:32,190 --> 00:22:34,150
with a norm on the operator.

417
00:22:34,150 --> 00:22:37,110
It's called induced operator norm.

418
00:22:37,110 --> 00:22:39,150
AUDIENCE: So the activation functions usually

419
00:22:39,150 --> 00:22:41,230
don't have weights.

420
00:22:41,230 --> 00:22:42,270
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes.

421
00:22:42,270 --> 00:22:44,905
AUDIENCE: So, I guess, what are we norming?

422
00:22:44,905 --> 00:22:47,030
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: So when I talk about activations,

423
00:22:47,030 --> 00:22:49,030
I just meant the feature vectors.

424
00:22:49,030 --> 00:22:50,630
But then if you think about--

425
00:22:54,350 --> 00:22:56,170
yeah, so I just meant the feature vectors.

426
00:22:56,170 --> 00:23:03,590
But when it comes to including-- if you want to think of a ReLU

427
00:23:03,590 --> 00:23:06,950
nonlinearity as a LEGO block, it will just be a LEGO block

428
00:23:06,950 --> 00:23:10,090
with the weight space is just the 0 vector space,

429
00:23:10,090 --> 00:23:11,850
or it just has a trivial weight space.

430
00:23:17,700 --> 00:23:21,860
But we really need a norm not on each individual layer

431
00:23:21,860 --> 00:23:23,860
separately, although that's kind of already

432
00:23:23,860 --> 00:23:26,235
an interesting thing to think about is what norm would we

433
00:23:26,235 --> 00:23:28,180
give to each individual layer, but we really

434
00:23:28,180 --> 00:23:30,300
want a norm on the full weight vector.

435
00:23:30,300 --> 00:23:32,460
So we really need a procedure for taking

436
00:23:32,460 --> 00:23:35,420
all of these layer-wise norms and combining them

437
00:23:35,420 --> 00:23:41,220
into one meganorm and on the whole neural net.

438
00:23:41,220 --> 00:23:44,920
So that's what we tried to figure out how to do.

439
00:23:44,920 --> 00:23:49,420
So there's this idea of starting with very granular norms

440
00:23:49,420 --> 00:23:52,820
and then combining them until we have one norm that describes

441
00:23:52,820 --> 00:23:54,000
the full weight space.

442
00:23:58,780 --> 00:24:00,500
So part two of the talk is I'm going

443
00:24:00,500 --> 00:24:05,340
to try to make this more like a formal procedure.

444
00:24:05,340 --> 00:24:10,060
So we call it the theory of modules.

445
00:24:10,060 --> 00:24:13,760
And so this is another perspective on this LEGO idea.

446
00:24:13,760 --> 00:24:15,930
But there's something called combinator pattern

447
00:24:15,930 --> 00:24:21,050
in programming, which is the idea of building

448
00:24:21,050 --> 00:24:24,010
complex structures by taking simple things

449
00:24:24,010 --> 00:24:25,510
and combining them.

450
00:24:25,510 --> 00:24:27,670
And you think that there's an abstract type,

451
00:24:27,670 --> 00:24:29,433
and you can build very simple things which

452
00:24:29,433 --> 00:24:30,850
are members of that type, and then

453
00:24:30,850 --> 00:24:32,725
you combine them and you get new things which

454
00:24:32,725 --> 00:24:35,850
belong to that type.

455
00:24:35,850 --> 00:24:38,650
So for us, the abstract type is something

456
00:24:38,650 --> 00:24:42,130
called a module, which has inputs weights and an output.

457
00:24:42,130 --> 00:24:45,470
And that can be an individual layer in a neural network,

458
00:24:45,470 --> 00:24:47,210
or it can be a whole neural network

459
00:24:47,210 --> 00:24:50,270
that both of those things would be modules.

460
00:24:52,810 --> 00:24:56,870
And then given two modules, we have these combination rules.

461
00:24:56,870 --> 00:25:01,490
So there's composition, which is like series, combination.

462
00:25:01,490 --> 00:25:04,050
And concatenation is the name that we

463
00:25:04,050 --> 00:25:06,430
gave to parallel combination.

464
00:25:10,130 --> 00:25:12,008
And again, it's just like the LEGO bricks.

465
00:25:12,008 --> 00:25:13,800
It's like putting them on top of each other

466
00:25:13,800 --> 00:25:15,300
or putting them next to each other.

467
00:25:18,120 --> 00:25:22,440
And this is just to show you actually you

468
00:25:22,440 --> 00:25:25,000
can build interesting little--

469
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:26,700
you can think of them also as circuits.

470
00:25:26,700 --> 00:25:29,000
But if you want to add two modules,

471
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:31,680
you can put them in parallel, and then you can compose them

472
00:25:31,680 --> 00:25:33,840
with an addition module.

473
00:25:33,840 --> 00:25:37,280
So it's like a kind of little circuit.

474
00:25:37,280 --> 00:25:39,360
Scalar multiplication-- you can take a module

475
00:25:39,360 --> 00:25:41,920
and you can compose it with a special module, which

476
00:25:41,920 --> 00:25:43,620
just multiplies by scalar.

477
00:25:47,320 --> 00:25:50,080
And then you can combine these things

478
00:25:50,080 --> 00:25:52,602
to build a residual block.

479
00:25:52,602 --> 00:25:54,060
So it kind of just looks like this.

480
00:25:54,060 --> 00:26:01,180
So it's like some concatenations and compositions.

481
00:26:01,180 --> 00:26:02,680
But essentially, then, you can think

482
00:26:02,680 --> 00:26:05,440
about taking that little circuit and composing it with itself

483
00:26:05,440 --> 00:26:09,600
a bunch of times to build a residual network.

484
00:26:09,600 --> 00:26:11,140
And this is just to give you an idea

485
00:26:11,140 --> 00:26:14,420
that actually those simple kind of binary combination rules

486
00:26:14,420 --> 00:26:17,180
are actually enough to build all the neural networks

487
00:26:17,180 --> 00:26:18,200
that you want to build.

488
00:26:22,460 --> 00:26:26,900
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE], I guess we're adding [INAUDIBLE].

489
00:26:26,900 --> 00:26:28,860
I guess we could just have modules

490
00:26:28,860 --> 00:26:31,540
that have two inputs then?

491
00:26:31,540 --> 00:26:33,460
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, a module having

492
00:26:33,460 --> 00:26:35,860
two inputs we would think of as the input as being

493
00:26:35,860 --> 00:26:38,180
a single tuple.

494
00:26:38,180 --> 00:26:41,180
So the input space can have whatever structure you want.

495
00:26:41,180 --> 00:26:43,980
AUDIENCE: Just concatenate operational [INAUDIBLE]?

496
00:26:43,980 --> 00:26:46,480
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, so it just has to be a vector space,

497
00:26:46,480 --> 00:26:48,120
but in the abstract sense.

498
00:26:55,060 --> 00:26:59,020
So this is to say that we can build--

499
00:26:59,020 --> 00:27:00,860
then we have this kind of categorization

500
00:27:00,860 --> 00:27:04,280
of different types of modules that we find interesting.

501
00:27:04,280 --> 00:27:11,570
So atoms-- this is the name that we give to the simple layers

502
00:27:11,570 --> 00:27:14,290
that you write by hand, like a linear layer, a convolution

503
00:27:14,290 --> 00:27:16,890
layer, an embedding layer.

504
00:27:16,890 --> 00:27:20,190
Bonds are just the ones with the kind of trivial weight space.

505
00:27:20,190 --> 00:27:23,850
So things like nonlinearities don't have weights.

506
00:27:23,850 --> 00:27:27,370
And a functional attention is the part

507
00:27:27,370 --> 00:27:29,450
of attention without the weights.

508
00:27:29,450 --> 00:27:33,170
And then you could compose it with some linear modules

509
00:27:33,170 --> 00:27:37,090
and so on if you want to build an actual attention module.

510
00:27:37,090 --> 00:27:40,330
And then compounds is the name that we give to anything

511
00:27:40,330 --> 00:27:42,350
you build by combinations.

512
00:27:44,970 --> 00:27:47,130
And this is just a recap that what we

513
00:27:47,130 --> 00:27:49,625
really what we've said that-- the thing that we care about

514
00:27:49,625 --> 00:27:51,250
or the thing that we want to know about

515
00:27:51,250 --> 00:27:53,350
is the sensitivity information.

516
00:27:53,350 --> 00:27:57,090
If I change the inputs and I change the weights,

517
00:27:57,090 --> 00:27:59,150
how much does the output change?

518
00:27:59,150 --> 00:28:00,570
Can we predict how much the output

519
00:28:00,570 --> 00:28:02,290
changes from knowing how much we change

520
00:28:02,290 --> 00:28:04,970
the inputs and the weights?

521
00:28:04,970 --> 00:28:07,760
And can we do this in particular for any module?

522
00:28:07,760 --> 00:28:10,520
So let's suppose that we can do it for the basic building

523
00:28:10,520 --> 00:28:11,440
blocks.

524
00:28:11,440 --> 00:28:13,600
Can we do it for any compound module

525
00:28:13,600 --> 00:28:19,380
or anything built from our LEGO set

526
00:28:19,380 --> 00:28:20,940
is the analogy we were making.

527
00:28:29,360 --> 00:28:32,700
So I worked recently with a pure mathematician, basically.

528
00:28:32,700 --> 00:28:35,600
So it's quite an abstract or formal way

529
00:28:35,600 --> 00:28:37,420
of going about solving this problem.

530
00:28:37,420 --> 00:28:42,140
But essentially, we've come up with these definitions.

531
00:28:42,140 --> 00:28:43,640
So just to remind you, a module is

532
00:28:43,640 --> 00:28:46,160
something with inputs, weights, and an output.

533
00:28:46,160 --> 00:28:50,040
But we also give it some metadata,

534
00:28:50,040 --> 00:28:54,340
like its forward function, a sensitivity number,

535
00:28:54,340 --> 00:28:58,180
which is supposed to measure the input-output sensitivity.

536
00:28:58,180 --> 00:29:02,060
So it's one of the Lipschitz constants.

537
00:29:02,060 --> 00:29:04,470
The mass is supposed to be a kind of hyperparameter which

538
00:29:04,470 --> 00:29:07,770
sets how important this module is compared to other modules,

539
00:29:07,770 --> 00:29:10,110
how much it will contribute to learning.

540
00:29:10,110 --> 00:29:11,950
And then we're asking that it should have

541
00:29:11,950 --> 00:29:14,750
a norm on the weight space.

542
00:29:14,750 --> 00:29:19,590
And anything with these attributes is a valid module

543
00:29:19,590 --> 00:29:22,910
but because, in our library, we don't

544
00:29:22,910 --> 00:29:24,610
want to have really horrible functions,

545
00:29:24,610 --> 00:29:27,890
we want to have nice functions that people can use,

546
00:29:27,890 --> 00:29:30,830
and so that's why we have this second definition, which

547
00:29:30,830 --> 00:29:35,670
is a module being well-normed.

548
00:29:35,670 --> 00:29:37,883
So these are the good modules, the ones

549
00:29:37,883 --> 00:29:39,550
that we actually like to use and that we

550
00:29:39,550 --> 00:29:43,390
want to include in our library.

551
00:29:43,390 --> 00:29:45,090
So for a module to be well-normed,

552
00:29:45,090 --> 00:29:48,550
it needs to have a notion of a norm on its input space

553
00:29:48,550 --> 00:29:54,030
and a norm on its output space, which corresponds to roughly

554
00:29:54,030 --> 00:29:56,310
saying you want to know how the inputs behave

555
00:29:56,310 --> 00:29:58,190
and how the outputs behave, or how

556
00:29:58,190 --> 00:30:01,550
you want the outputs to behave.

557
00:30:01,550 --> 00:30:08,130
And then we'll basically say that a module is well-normed

558
00:30:08,130 --> 00:30:12,170
if it's one Lipschitz in its weights

559
00:30:12,170 --> 00:30:14,390
and if it's Lipschitz in its inputs

560
00:30:14,390 --> 00:30:16,730
where the sensitivity number assigned to the module

561
00:30:16,730 --> 00:30:19,822
is Lipschitz constant.

562
00:30:19,822 --> 00:30:21,530
So the way that we're thinking about this

563
00:30:21,530 --> 00:30:25,330
is like when we say that a LEGO brick has a theory associated

564
00:30:25,330 --> 00:30:28,530
with it, it basically means that a LEGO

565
00:30:28,530 --> 00:30:31,890
brick has these attributes.

566
00:30:31,890 --> 00:30:35,530
And the second definition says that the attributes

567
00:30:35,530 --> 00:30:38,570
are based on constraints with respect to each other.

568
00:30:38,570 --> 00:30:42,250
And if the LEGO brick satisfies these constraints,

569
00:30:42,250 --> 00:30:43,417
we'll put it in our library.

570
00:30:43,417 --> 00:30:45,167
And if it doesn't, we'll just throw it out

571
00:30:45,167 --> 00:30:46,270
because we don't like it.

572
00:30:49,330 --> 00:30:52,250
So I want to give you some examples of what this actually

573
00:30:52,250 --> 00:30:53,030
looks like.

574
00:30:53,030 --> 00:30:56,090
So these are some--

575
00:30:56,090 --> 00:31:00,720
first of all, the linear module, the most classic

576
00:31:00,720 --> 00:31:03,160
does a matrix vector multiplication.

577
00:31:03,160 --> 00:31:07,080
We'll say that it has sensitivity 1 and mass 1.

578
00:31:07,080 --> 00:31:09,840
And we'll give it this kind of funky norm,

579
00:31:09,840 --> 00:31:12,100
this rescaled spectral norm.

580
00:31:14,800 --> 00:31:18,040
And then we need to ask, is this module well-normed or not?

581
00:31:18,040 --> 00:31:20,140
Do we want to put it into our library or not?

582
00:31:23,160 --> 00:31:26,400
And so the claim is that if you set the input and output

583
00:31:26,400 --> 00:31:28,160
norm to be the RMS norm, which is kind

584
00:31:28,160 --> 00:31:31,680
of a natural choice for feature vectors inside a neural network,

585
00:31:31,680 --> 00:31:33,640
then the linear module is well-normed

586
00:31:33,640 --> 00:31:37,220
if it has bounded inputs and if the weights are bounded.

587
00:31:39,840 --> 00:31:43,140
So yeah, I'm still kind of thinking about this topic,

588
00:31:43,140 --> 00:31:44,920
but it's kind of interesting that one

589
00:31:44,920 --> 00:31:48,040
of the basic things that, in an actual neural net, that you do

590
00:31:48,040 --> 00:31:52,900
is you normalize all the feature vectors everywhere explicitly.

591
00:31:52,900 --> 00:31:54,400
People don't really know why they're

592
00:31:54,400 --> 00:31:57,220
doing that other than somehow, intuitively,

593
00:31:57,220 --> 00:31:58,950
it's going to make things more stable

594
00:31:58,950 --> 00:32:00,430
and stop things blowing up.

595
00:32:00,430 --> 00:32:02,270
But this is pointing out that if you

596
00:32:02,270 --> 00:32:05,230
want to have Lipschitz guarantees for a linear module,

597
00:32:05,230 --> 00:32:07,830
the inputs and the weights must be bounded.

598
00:32:07,830 --> 00:32:11,010
If they're not, it can be arbitrarily sensitive.

599
00:32:17,030 --> 00:32:20,790
So a useful counterpoint is an embedding module

600
00:32:20,790 --> 00:32:22,643
because, at first glance, it looks

601
00:32:22,643 --> 00:32:24,810
like it's kind of the same thing as a linear module.

602
00:32:24,810 --> 00:32:29,910
You can write it as a matrix vector multiplication.

603
00:32:29,910 --> 00:32:31,470
But the difference is that you think

604
00:32:31,470 --> 00:32:33,750
about an embedding layer in a neural network

605
00:32:33,750 --> 00:32:36,790
is you pass in a one hot vector, and it picks out

606
00:32:36,790 --> 00:32:38,610
one of the columns of the matrix,

607
00:32:38,610 --> 00:32:42,190
and that's the embedding that corresponds to that token.

608
00:32:42,190 --> 00:32:45,350
So it has an embedding layer has a very different semantics

609
00:32:45,350 --> 00:32:48,230
to a linear layer.

610
00:32:48,230 --> 00:32:52,350
And one of the ways that gets expressed

611
00:32:52,350 --> 00:32:54,950
is by giving the weight space of an embedding module

612
00:32:54,950 --> 00:33:00,540
a different norm to a linear module.

613
00:33:00,540 --> 00:33:03,820
And similarly, when we ask if the module is well-normed

614
00:33:03,820 --> 00:33:07,000
or not, we'll put a different norm on the input space.

615
00:33:07,000 --> 00:33:08,900
And the way we think about this is

616
00:33:08,900 --> 00:33:11,300
that the input vector to an embedding layer

617
00:33:11,300 --> 00:33:14,560
is a one hot vector, which is a bit like a probability vector.

618
00:33:14,560 --> 00:33:16,120
So we give it the L1 norm.

619
00:33:19,820 --> 00:33:22,380
And then it's basically with that choice

620
00:33:22,380 --> 00:33:25,700
of input norm and output norm.

621
00:33:25,700 --> 00:33:29,340
It's well-known that under the same conditions of like unit

622
00:33:29,340 --> 00:33:32,300
inputs and unit weights.

623
00:33:32,300 --> 00:33:35,540
AUDIENCE: Actually, I just finished trying to figure out

624
00:33:35,540 --> 00:33:38,860
the well-normed module definition before--

625
00:33:38,860 --> 00:33:41,660
there's a diamond, like a rotated square.

626
00:33:41,660 --> 00:33:42,500
What does that mean?

627
00:33:42,500 --> 00:33:45,980
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: That means contract over any tensor indices

628
00:33:45,980 --> 00:33:48,380
which are shared between the--

629
00:33:48,380 --> 00:33:51,605
it's just like a tensor contraction.

630
00:33:51,605 --> 00:33:52,480
Does that make sense?

631
00:33:52,480 --> 00:33:56,190
It's like if there are two vectors, it's a dot product.

632
00:33:56,190 --> 00:33:57,770
If it's a matrix and a vector, it's

633
00:33:57,770 --> 00:33:59,890
a matrix vector multiplication.

634
00:33:59,890 --> 00:34:02,830
If it's a matrix and a matrix where the indices are the same,

635
00:34:02,830 --> 00:34:05,770
it's like sum over the [INAUDIBLE] indices.

636
00:34:05,770 --> 00:34:10,889
It's just like the objects in a neural network

637
00:34:10,889 --> 00:34:13,150
have a whole zoo of different shapes.

638
00:34:13,150 --> 00:34:17,710
And it's hard to come up with a notation to express that.

639
00:34:17,710 --> 00:34:19,409
So it's just like a shorthand for figure

640
00:34:19,409 --> 00:34:22,150
out what kind of multiplication you actually want to do here.

641
00:34:27,530 --> 00:34:30,090
AUDIENCE: Do you not run into issues

642
00:34:30,090 --> 00:34:33,810
when you optimize this with boundary value problems,

643
00:34:33,810 --> 00:34:37,969
where, let's say, you're at the boundary of your input space

644
00:34:37,969 --> 00:34:41,370
being less than or equal to 1 and your gradient is

645
00:34:41,370 --> 00:34:45,150
like trying to push you outside of the boundary?

646
00:34:45,150 --> 00:34:47,150
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, that's a great question.

647
00:34:47,150 --> 00:34:50,810
So that the short answer is we've

648
00:34:50,810 --> 00:34:53,750
not actually implemented this part really faithfully yet.

649
00:34:53,750 --> 00:34:55,889
So when we actually implement it at the moment,

650
00:34:55,889 --> 00:34:58,130
we just let everything go outside the boundary.

651
00:34:58,130 --> 00:35:00,990
But I want to do that.

652
00:35:00,990 --> 00:35:04,030
And the idea is to design like manifold optimization algorithms

653
00:35:04,030 --> 00:35:08,910
where the weights stay on this like well-normed manifold.

654
00:35:08,910 --> 00:35:10,950
And then, because of that, that gives you

655
00:35:10,950 --> 00:35:12,617
guarantees about how the outputs behave.

656
00:35:12,617 --> 00:35:14,617
And then you can think about those as the inputs

657
00:35:14,617 --> 00:35:15,750
to the next layer.

658
00:35:15,750 --> 00:35:18,090
And there's a way to make everything consistent.

659
00:35:18,090 --> 00:35:19,890
We just haven't done it yet.

660
00:35:27,510 --> 00:35:31,590
So yeah, so far, it's different aspects of the whole theory

661
00:35:31,590 --> 00:35:33,810
seem to be useful.

662
00:35:33,810 --> 00:35:36,130
But there's not like a complete version,

663
00:35:36,130 --> 00:35:38,770
which is where everything is consistent in practice.

664
00:35:44,230 --> 00:35:46,710
So the reason for setting up those abstract definitions

665
00:35:46,710 --> 00:35:49,750
of what is a module and what is a well-normed module

666
00:35:49,750 --> 00:35:54,240
is that we want to have combination rules which

667
00:35:54,240 --> 00:35:56,680
somehow automatically preserve those properties.

668
00:35:56,680 --> 00:35:59,460
So if you take two well-normed modules in your library

669
00:35:59,460 --> 00:36:02,180
and compose them, the new thing that you get

670
00:36:02,180 --> 00:36:07,980
is automatically satisfies the definition of being well normed.

671
00:36:07,980 --> 00:36:10,660
And so the types of things that we want to show

672
00:36:10,660 --> 00:36:13,120
are that the combination rules are associative.

673
00:36:13,120 --> 00:36:16,820
So if you do compose a chain of things,

674
00:36:16,820 --> 00:36:18,840
you get the same fundamental object,

675
00:36:18,840 --> 00:36:23,620
whichever order you take those compositions, for example.

676
00:36:23,620 --> 00:36:26,460
We want the space of modules to be kind of closed

677
00:36:26,460 --> 00:36:31,620
under combinations, the space of well-normed modules

678
00:36:31,620 --> 00:36:34,820
to be closed under combinations.

679
00:36:34,820 --> 00:36:37,780
And then we want that mass parameter that one of those

680
00:36:37,780 --> 00:36:41,140
attributes to allow us to tune how

681
00:36:41,140 --> 00:36:43,180
much different modules contribute relative

682
00:36:43,180 --> 00:36:43,920
to each other.

683
00:36:43,920 --> 00:36:46,860
And intuitively think about that as giving you

684
00:36:46,860 --> 00:36:49,510
the freedom to tune learning rates at different layers

685
00:36:49,510 --> 00:36:50,990
if you want to do that.

686
00:36:53,530 --> 00:36:58,190
And so we came up with these rules.

687
00:36:58,190 --> 00:37:00,330
So again, these are just definitions.

688
00:37:00,330 --> 00:37:03,190
And the kind of style of what this is,

689
00:37:03,190 --> 00:37:05,910
is come up with really, really clever definitions.

690
00:37:05,910 --> 00:37:09,330
And then everything else is easy.

691
00:37:09,330 --> 00:37:13,250
So the definitions are kind of the hard part.

692
00:37:13,250 --> 00:37:19,570
So for composing modules or putting modules in series,

693
00:37:19,570 --> 00:37:21,270
you compose the forward functions,

694
00:37:21,270 --> 00:37:23,130
which seems kind of natural.

695
00:37:23,130 --> 00:37:24,810
The sensitivity, which is supposed

696
00:37:24,810 --> 00:37:28,190
to be the input to output Lipschitz constant,

697
00:37:28,190 --> 00:37:31,330
those multiply, which is kind of like if you compose things,

698
00:37:31,330 --> 00:37:33,190
you multiply that Lipschitz constant.

699
00:37:33,190 --> 00:37:35,370
So that's maybe intuitive.

700
00:37:35,370 --> 00:37:38,970
Mass is like, you have to think about it a little more.

701
00:37:38,970 --> 00:37:43,270
And then the important thing is that we use a max norm.

702
00:37:43,270 --> 00:37:45,050
So when we compose modules, we want

703
00:37:45,050 --> 00:37:47,940
to have a norm on the joint weight space.

704
00:37:47,940 --> 00:37:51,360
Then we pick the max norm with a special weighting.

705
00:37:51,360 --> 00:37:54,200
So there's coefficients p and q, which

706
00:37:54,200 --> 00:37:57,800
weight the two different norms in the max.

707
00:37:57,800 --> 00:38:01,440
And p and q depend on the mass ratios, which that's

708
00:38:01,440 --> 00:38:03,080
the idea that the mass allows you

709
00:38:03,080 --> 00:38:04,800
to tune the importance of the two

710
00:38:04,800 --> 00:38:07,040
different modules in the composition,

711
00:38:07,040 --> 00:38:11,520
and the scalar p also couples to the sensitivity

712
00:38:11,520 --> 00:38:12,920
of the second module.

713
00:38:12,920 --> 00:38:15,780
So that's like, if you compose two things,

714
00:38:15,780 --> 00:38:18,880
but the second one is very, very sensitive to its inputs.

715
00:38:18,880 --> 00:38:21,065
Somehow, the first one should know about that

716
00:38:21,065 --> 00:38:22,440
because if you're going to change

717
00:38:22,440 --> 00:38:24,920
the weights of the first one, you hopefully

718
00:38:24,920 --> 00:38:27,440
want to know that the second one is really, really sensitive

719
00:38:27,440 --> 00:38:29,000
or it's very insensitive.

720
00:38:29,000 --> 00:38:33,320
So it's like just another view on really trying

721
00:38:33,320 --> 00:38:35,920
to characterize the sensitivity of how things behave when you

722
00:38:35,920 --> 00:38:37,340
combine them with each other.

723
00:38:40,040 --> 00:38:44,320
AUDIENCE: So we explained the intuition for sensitivity

724
00:38:44,320 --> 00:38:45,900
in terms of [? functions. ?] What's

725
00:38:45,900 --> 00:38:48,460
the main intuition for mass?

726
00:38:48,460 --> 00:38:50,500
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, the intuition for mass

727
00:38:50,500 --> 00:38:53,840
is if you just forget about all of this

728
00:38:53,840 --> 00:38:56,435
and you say you're going to train a transformer

729
00:38:56,435 --> 00:38:58,560
and you think about the structure of a transformer,

730
00:38:58,560 --> 00:39:01,180
it's got like the kind of body of the network

731
00:39:01,180 --> 00:39:03,440
is like residual blocks.

732
00:39:03,440 --> 00:39:05,320
There's an embedding layer at the beginning,

733
00:39:05,320 --> 00:39:07,358
and there's a linear layer at the output.

734
00:39:07,358 --> 00:39:08,900
And then you say like, OK, now I want

735
00:39:08,900 --> 00:39:12,300
to scale the number of blocks.

736
00:39:12,300 --> 00:39:13,980
And let's say you want to even scale

737
00:39:13,980 --> 00:39:17,820
the number of blocks to the point where there's millions

738
00:39:17,820 --> 00:39:20,900
of blocks, but there's only one embedding layer and one output

739
00:39:20,900 --> 00:39:23,100
layer.

740
00:39:23,100 --> 00:39:26,500
The claim is that it's not obvious whether in the limit

741
00:39:26,500 --> 00:39:28,658
of a really large number of blocks.

742
00:39:28,658 --> 00:39:30,700
Should you even still be training the input layer

743
00:39:30,700 --> 00:39:31,520
and the output layer?

744
00:39:31,520 --> 00:39:32,860
Should they now become negligible

745
00:39:32,860 --> 00:39:33,980
because you want all the learning

746
00:39:33,980 --> 00:39:35,140
to happen in the middle?

747
00:39:35,140 --> 00:39:37,540
Or actually, the embedding layer plays

748
00:39:37,540 --> 00:39:39,620
a really interesting and different functional

749
00:39:39,620 --> 00:39:42,000
role to the blocks, so even if you have a lot of blocks,

750
00:39:42,000 --> 00:39:43,630
you still want to train the embeddings.

751
00:39:43,630 --> 00:39:45,950
It's not obvious what the answer to that question is.

752
00:39:45,950 --> 00:39:50,410
I find it difficult to know.

753
00:39:50,410 --> 00:39:52,430
So that's why there's a degree of freedom,

754
00:39:52,430 --> 00:39:54,930
which is letting you tune how much you

755
00:39:54,930 --> 00:39:56,570
want the embedding to be important

756
00:39:56,570 --> 00:39:58,970
relative to the blocks.

757
00:39:58,970 --> 00:40:02,150
AUDIENCE: So it's being defined as the thing that you add up,

758
00:40:02,150 --> 00:40:05,330
regardless of whether it's composition or concatenation.

759
00:40:05,330 --> 00:40:09,290
So in that case, could you say if you had a set of blocks,

760
00:40:09,290 --> 00:40:10,970
and you're trying to analyze it, and you

761
00:40:10,970 --> 00:40:12,850
doubled all of their masses, would that

762
00:40:12,850 --> 00:40:14,890
give you the same analysis?

763
00:40:14,890 --> 00:40:18,210
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, it would.

764
00:40:18,210 --> 00:40:19,270
Analysis goes through.

765
00:40:19,270 --> 00:40:22,010
But it would just mean that when you train the network

766
00:40:22,010 --> 00:40:24,970
under this norm, using this norm to help you train it,

767
00:40:24,970 --> 00:40:28,010
that the amount of contribution towards learning of the blocks

768
00:40:28,010 --> 00:40:31,690
would double compared to the things whose mass you

769
00:40:31,690 --> 00:40:32,858
don't double.

770
00:40:32,858 --> 00:40:34,650
AUDIENCE: Oh, but if there's no other thing

771
00:40:34,650 --> 00:40:35,710
that you don't double--

772
00:40:35,710 --> 00:40:37,335
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Then nothing changes.

773
00:40:37,335 --> 00:40:37,890
Exactly.

774
00:40:41,720 --> 00:40:43,935
AUDIENCE: Follow-up question on this.

775
00:40:43,935 --> 00:40:46,060
How do you determine the mass for different blocks?

776
00:40:46,060 --> 00:40:49,360
So I am assuming here that mass incorporates

777
00:40:49,360 --> 00:40:53,960
some-- defines the richness of a certain block in terms

778
00:40:53,960 --> 00:40:56,780
of its feature representation or ability?

779
00:40:56,780 --> 00:40:58,280
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: What it determines

780
00:40:58,280 --> 00:41:01,160
is how much that block during training

781
00:41:01,160 --> 00:41:03,800
is going to influence the features of anything

782
00:41:03,800 --> 00:41:06,520
that it contributes to, any kind of super module

783
00:41:06,520 --> 00:41:09,200
that it contributes to.

784
00:41:09,200 --> 00:41:12,360
And sort of like a degree of freedom

785
00:41:12,360 --> 00:41:14,860
that we couldn't really see a theoretical way to get rid of,

786
00:41:14,860 --> 00:41:17,720
so it's something you have to tune.

787
00:41:17,720 --> 00:41:19,340
But when we actually did experiments,

788
00:41:19,340 --> 00:41:24,180
we just tuned the mass of all of the blocks together.

789
00:41:24,180 --> 00:41:26,080
So we give each--

790
00:41:26,080 --> 00:41:28,820
if all of the blocks have mass m and there's l blocks,

791
00:41:28,820 --> 00:41:31,960
each block gets mass m divided by l.

792
00:41:31,960 --> 00:41:34,500
So there's one number, which is the mass of all the blocks.

793
00:41:34,500 --> 00:41:37,080
And then we fix the input layer to have mass 1 and the output

794
00:41:37,080 --> 00:41:39,080
layer to have mass 1.

795
00:41:39,080 --> 00:41:42,270
AUDIENCE: So if you have a block that's linear that has mass 1,

796
00:41:42,270 --> 00:41:44,570
and you have a block as a convolution,

797
00:41:44,570 --> 00:41:46,710
that would also have the same--

798
00:41:46,710 --> 00:41:47,990
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes.

799
00:41:47,990 --> 00:41:51,230
And we do that for simplicity because it's

800
00:41:51,230 --> 00:41:55,670
a pain if you-- but the current understanding is maybe

801
00:41:55,670 --> 00:41:58,210
it would work better if you tune them separately.

802
00:41:58,210 --> 00:42:04,510
But you can tune them in groups if you want to.

803
00:42:04,510 --> 00:42:06,730
So concatenation is kind of analogous,

804
00:42:06,730 --> 00:42:09,070
but it doesn't have the coupling because they

805
00:42:09,070 --> 00:42:10,530
don't compose with each other.

806
00:42:10,530 --> 00:42:12,750
So it's a little bit simpler.

807
00:42:12,750 --> 00:42:16,310
But the point of the point of these definitions

808
00:42:16,310 --> 00:42:19,410
is that you take these definitions.

809
00:42:19,410 --> 00:42:21,290
You take the definition of well-normedness,

810
00:42:21,290 --> 00:42:24,470
and you can prove that if you compose two things that

811
00:42:24,470 --> 00:42:27,530
are well-normed, then the composition is also well-normed.

812
00:42:27,530 --> 00:42:29,830
So they preserve that property.

813
00:42:29,830 --> 00:42:32,350
And they have another-- there's another advantage which

814
00:42:32,350 --> 00:42:37,770
is that you can work this theory through to second order.

815
00:42:37,770 --> 00:42:40,050
So you define what you mean by the sharpness.

816
00:42:40,050 --> 00:42:44,210
It's a bit like the reciprocal of the smoothness.

817
00:42:44,210 --> 00:42:48,130
But you say that the module is alpha, beta, gamma sharp

818
00:42:48,130 --> 00:42:50,570
if it's second derivatives.

819
00:42:50,570 --> 00:42:53,310
In other words, it's Lipschitz smooth

820
00:42:53,310 --> 00:42:55,290
if the module is Lipschitz smooth with respect

821
00:42:55,290 --> 00:42:57,410
to its weight norm and input norm.

822
00:42:57,410 --> 00:43:00,490
But because a module has two arguments,

823
00:43:00,490 --> 00:43:05,390
we can give it three sharpness coefficients,

824
00:43:05,390 --> 00:43:09,430
which correspond to varying just the weights,

825
00:43:09,430 --> 00:43:10,910
varying the weights and the input,

826
00:43:10,910 --> 00:43:12,150
or varying just the input.

827
00:43:12,150 --> 00:43:15,672
So that's why there's three sharpness coefficients.

828
00:43:15,672 --> 00:43:17,130
And you can think of these as being

829
00:43:17,130 --> 00:43:19,110
like generalized top eigenvalues.

830
00:43:19,110 --> 00:43:25,210
So oftentimes, when people talk about the sharpness

831
00:43:25,210 --> 00:43:28,930
of the Hessian in deep learning, what they're meaning

832
00:43:28,930 --> 00:43:32,710
is how big is the largest eigenvalue of the Hessian.

833
00:43:32,710 --> 00:43:35,690
But somehow, that's like a Euclidean constant.

834
00:43:35,690 --> 00:43:38,880
And if you don't believe that the geometry of the weight space

835
00:43:38,880 --> 00:43:41,640
is a Euclidean geometry, then you shouldn't necessarily even

836
00:43:41,640 --> 00:43:43,760
care about the top eigenvalue.

837
00:43:43,760 --> 00:43:48,000
And you need some kind of generalized notion.

838
00:43:48,000 --> 00:43:53,840
And the point is that if a module is well-normed and has

839
00:43:53,840 --> 00:43:56,640
these sharpness coefficients, there's an automatic way

840
00:43:56,640 --> 00:44:00,000
to determine what the sharpness coefficients are of compositions

841
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:02,680
and concatenations.

842
00:44:02,680 --> 00:44:05,160
And then you can use that to show that the overall loss

843
00:44:05,160 --> 00:44:13,660
function is Lipschitz smooth and some other things.

844
00:44:19,320 --> 00:44:27,200
So that was a lot of building a theory and not really clear

845
00:44:27,200 --> 00:44:30,080
if it's going to be useful.

846
00:44:30,080 --> 00:44:34,670
So the next two parts are talking about different ways

847
00:44:34,670 --> 00:44:39,510
that we've been trying to apply this theory.

848
00:44:39,510 --> 00:44:42,390
And in some sense, the theory is intended

849
00:44:42,390 --> 00:44:44,290
to try to solve these problems as well.

850
00:44:47,190 --> 00:44:49,290
So now I want to think about scaling.

851
00:44:49,290 --> 00:44:51,290
So you want to scale your training.

852
00:44:51,290 --> 00:44:54,310
You want to make your transformer as big as possible.

853
00:44:54,310 --> 00:44:56,910
And that's this idea, even in the natural world,

854
00:44:56,910 --> 00:44:59,570
that big things with bigger brains

855
00:44:59,570 --> 00:45:02,370
are supposed to be more intelligent.

856
00:45:02,370 --> 00:45:05,210
And the African elephant is, according to Wikipedia,

857
00:45:05,210 --> 00:45:09,070
is the organism with the most neurons.

858
00:45:09,070 --> 00:45:12,247
And then we've got this recipe for

859
00:45:12,247 --> 00:45:13,830
artificial general intelligence, where

860
00:45:13,830 --> 00:45:16,150
it's like, get the biggest supercomputer that you can

861
00:45:16,150 --> 00:45:18,270
and scrape all the data and then train

862
00:45:18,270 --> 00:45:21,830
the really big transformer.

863
00:45:21,830 --> 00:45:24,990
And that's this problem that we talked about it in the earlier

864
00:45:24,990 --> 00:45:28,350
lecture, but the training properties of the system

865
00:45:28,350 --> 00:45:30,910
can drift as you scale up.

866
00:45:30,910 --> 00:45:34,660
So even though with the case of width, even as the network gets

867
00:45:34,660 --> 00:45:37,680
wider, the performance is getting better,

868
00:45:37,680 --> 00:45:40,580
but the optimal step size, which is on the x-axis,

869
00:45:40,580 --> 00:45:42,580
is just drifting.

870
00:45:42,580 --> 00:45:47,218
And sometimes, if you scale badly in depth,

871
00:45:47,218 --> 00:45:49,760
the performance can get worse as you make the network deeper.

872
00:45:49,760 --> 00:45:51,260
So we want to try to have a way that

873
00:45:51,260 --> 00:45:54,900
solves some of these problems.

874
00:45:54,900 --> 00:46:00,020
So our thesis for good scaling-- why is scaling bad?

875
00:46:00,020 --> 00:46:04,600
Our thesis is scaling well is actually not difficult.

876
00:46:04,600 --> 00:46:09,220
It's just about really understanding the Lipschitz

877
00:46:09,220 --> 00:46:11,620
properties of the system, which is the thing that people

878
00:46:11,620 --> 00:46:14,900
don't really understand.

879
00:46:14,900 --> 00:46:19,500
And so the thesis is that if you have Lipschitz guarantees

880
00:46:19,500 --> 00:46:22,800
for your network which are tight,

881
00:46:22,800 --> 00:46:25,180
meaning that it's not like a really loose band that's

882
00:46:25,180 --> 00:46:28,580
kind of useless, and if the Lipschitz constants are

883
00:46:28,580 --> 00:46:33,200
non-dimensional, which basically means that as you change

884
00:46:33,200 --> 00:46:35,960
the scale of the system, the Lipschitz constant is staying

885
00:46:35,960 --> 00:46:41,520
fixed, it's not varying, then if you normalize the updates

886
00:46:41,520 --> 00:46:46,313
in the corresponding norm, that will give you good scaling.

887
00:46:46,313 --> 00:46:48,480
You kind of call it a thesis because it's not really

888
00:46:48,480 --> 00:46:49,060
a theorem.

889
00:46:49,060 --> 00:46:50,540
It's just like what we think.

890
00:46:54,480 --> 00:46:57,218
So we want to have Lipschitz constants

891
00:46:57,218 --> 00:46:58,760
for the overall neural network, which

892
00:46:58,760 --> 00:47:02,040
are independent of the scaling dimensions.

893
00:47:02,040 --> 00:47:04,280
And what we really want is to believe

894
00:47:04,280 --> 00:47:06,600
that the bounds that these Lipschitz bounds

895
00:47:06,600 --> 00:47:10,240
are going to be tight, and the amount of tightness

896
00:47:10,240 --> 00:47:12,700
that the bounds have is not dependent on scale.

897
00:47:16,440 --> 00:47:20,440
So then, if we can have that, then the norm

898
00:47:20,440 --> 00:47:25,600
on the overall module, which those bounds hold in.

899
00:47:25,600 --> 00:47:27,960
If we control that norm of the weight updates,

900
00:47:27,960 --> 00:47:30,750
we'll be able to predict how much the outputs change.

901
00:47:30,750 --> 00:47:34,130
So that's one of the reasons we were setting this thing up.

902
00:47:34,130 --> 00:47:40,110
So in other words, this is the example

903
00:47:40,110 --> 00:47:43,670
that we mainly think about, but if the neural network is

904
00:47:43,670 --> 00:47:46,570
1 Lipschitz, meaning that the Lipschitz constant is 1,

905
00:47:46,570 --> 00:47:48,750
so one is a really great number, which

906
00:47:48,750 --> 00:47:55,230
is an example of a number, which is nondimensional,

907
00:47:55,230 --> 00:48:02,130
then if we control the weight norm of our weight updates,

908
00:48:02,130 --> 00:48:04,030
we'll be able to predict that output

909
00:48:04,030 --> 00:48:06,370
norm of the change in outputs.

910
00:48:09,190 --> 00:48:14,950
And this is just showing you some of the papers

911
00:48:14,950 --> 00:48:17,170
where we wrote about some of this stuff.

912
00:48:17,170 --> 00:48:19,570
But we broke up the problem into these two pieces.

913
00:48:19,570 --> 00:48:21,750
What are the properties of an individual layer?

914
00:48:21,750 --> 00:48:26,820
And how do we keep them under composition and concatenation?

915
00:48:26,820 --> 00:48:29,340
And this is just saying that, actually, some of them

916
00:48:29,340 --> 00:48:31,900
put a little bit on the homework, this idea

917
00:48:31,900 --> 00:48:34,380
that this particular scaled spectral norm is

918
00:48:34,380 --> 00:48:37,180
a really good norm for getting Lipschitz

919
00:48:37,180 --> 00:48:39,280
guarantees on a linear layer.

920
00:48:43,500 --> 00:48:48,240
And this paper that I worked on with Philip and also

921
00:48:48,240 --> 00:48:49,740
with some other collaborators, which

922
00:48:49,740 --> 00:48:54,380
is about the idea of combining things.

923
00:48:54,380 --> 00:48:57,380
And we actually built this software package, which is still

924
00:48:57,380 --> 00:48:58,980
a work in progress, but it's the idea

925
00:48:58,980 --> 00:49:00,820
that all of those rules, everything

926
00:49:00,820 --> 00:49:04,000
that I was showing you, are things that can be programmed.

927
00:49:04,000 --> 00:49:05,960
So you can actually build this library.

928
00:49:05,960 --> 00:49:08,220
So you think about it as building this LEGO set,

929
00:49:08,220 --> 00:49:12,220
where you can combine-- so this is an example of building an MLP

930
00:49:12,220 --> 00:49:17,540
by combining layers and nonlinearities to get--

931
00:49:17,540 --> 00:49:22,220
this MLP would then be a compound module.

932
00:49:22,220 --> 00:49:25,370
And then you would automatically get its forward function

933
00:49:25,370 --> 00:49:28,610
through taking the composition, but you also

934
00:49:28,610 --> 00:49:30,110
get this normalized function.

935
00:49:30,110 --> 00:49:32,050
So when we compose things, we automatically

936
00:49:32,050 --> 00:49:33,630
build a norm on the weight space.

937
00:49:33,630 --> 00:49:37,330
And then we can use that norm to normalize.

938
00:49:37,330 --> 00:49:42,310
And so this is our simple programming example.

939
00:49:42,310 --> 00:49:45,670
And the claim is that because we did this clever normalization,

940
00:49:45,670 --> 00:49:47,130
the training will now scale better

941
00:49:47,130 --> 00:49:49,270
without needing to retrain the learning rate.

942
00:49:54,090 --> 00:49:58,330
And you can basically build it on top of different programming

943
00:49:58,330 --> 00:49:59,450
languages.

944
00:49:59,450 --> 00:50:03,330
It can be put on top of PyTorch.

945
00:50:03,330 --> 00:50:05,630
And this is some empirical evidence.

946
00:50:18,670 --> 00:50:22,390
What we're showing here is actual width sweep.

947
00:50:22,390 --> 00:50:25,030
So we're increasing the width of the network.

948
00:50:25,030 --> 00:50:31,950
And the left plot is just training nanoGPT,

949
00:50:31,950 --> 00:50:35,308
which is Karpathy's transformer-- sorry, go ahead.

950
00:50:35,308 --> 00:50:36,850
AUDIENCE: I didn't mean to interrupt.

951
00:50:36,850 --> 00:50:37,725
[INAUDIBLE] question.

952
00:50:43,312 --> 00:50:45,270
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: The point is that if you just

953
00:50:45,270 --> 00:50:48,930
take like nanoGPT, which is this GitHub repository,

954
00:50:48,930 --> 00:50:51,550
and you try changing the width, the optimal learning

955
00:50:51,550 --> 00:50:54,150
rate is going to drift.

956
00:50:54,150 --> 00:50:57,450
The middle plot is our own transformer implementation,

957
00:50:57,450 --> 00:51:00,110
which has some tweaks to the architecture to make it actually

958
00:51:00,110 --> 00:51:02,710
Lipschitz.

959
00:51:02,710 --> 00:51:06,550
And then the third plot is adding normalization

960
00:51:06,550 --> 00:51:10,310
on top of the optimization.

961
00:51:10,310 --> 00:51:11,990
And the point is, this is supposed

962
00:51:11,990 --> 00:51:15,150
to be saying that by adding this normalization, we kind fix

963
00:51:15,150 --> 00:51:18,710
the drift in the learning rate.

964
00:51:18,710 --> 00:51:21,820
AUDIENCE: So I guess from the perspective of a gradient that

965
00:51:21,820 --> 00:51:25,780
has not been normalized, when you apply normalization,

966
00:51:25,780 --> 00:51:29,460
it's as if you are changing the learning rate.

967
00:51:29,460 --> 00:51:32,700
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes.

968
00:51:32,700 --> 00:51:34,943
Yeah, that's the one perspective.

969
00:51:34,943 --> 00:51:37,360
AUDIENCE: I guess it would be like changing learning rate,

970
00:51:37,360 --> 00:51:39,777
but in a way that's functionally dependent on the gradient

971
00:51:39,777 --> 00:51:40,540
itself.

972
00:51:40,540 --> 00:51:42,260
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yes.

973
00:51:42,260 --> 00:51:44,380
And it changes the learning rate in a way

974
00:51:44,380 --> 00:51:47,220
which depends on what the architecture of the network is.

975
00:51:47,220 --> 00:51:49,740
So depending on-- different layers

976
00:51:49,740 --> 00:51:52,380
will get gradients rescaled by different amounts.

977
00:51:52,380 --> 00:51:54,780
AUDIENCE: So learning rate here is a scaling

978
00:51:54,780 --> 00:51:55,822
of that entire process.

979
00:51:55,822 --> 00:51:56,780
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah.

980
00:52:00,780 --> 00:52:06,140
And this is to say that things also work in scaling depth.

981
00:52:06,140 --> 00:52:10,340
So the baseline nanoGPT performance-- it kind of is not

982
00:52:10,340 --> 00:52:11,920
so bad if you just scale depth.

983
00:52:11,920 --> 00:52:15,320
It works reasonably well, but it's a little bit messier.

984
00:52:15,320 --> 00:52:19,370
And this is just to say that-- so in the middle plot, which

985
00:52:19,370 --> 00:52:21,750
is without applying our normalization procedure,

986
00:52:21,750 --> 00:52:26,130
Adam coincidentally does pretty well in terms of depth scaling,

987
00:52:26,130 --> 00:52:28,270
and then adding the normalization on top,

988
00:52:28,270 --> 00:52:30,670
things stay pretty much the same, actually.

989
00:52:36,650 --> 00:52:38,850
So this is good because this is pretty much

990
00:52:38,850 --> 00:52:41,190
what we built this whole thing to be able to do.

991
00:52:41,190 --> 00:52:43,650
So it's kind of we knew these things were going

992
00:52:43,650 --> 00:52:46,290
to work because we had an intuitive understanding of how

993
00:52:46,290 --> 00:52:49,210
to do scaling, and then we kind of built the theory in order

994
00:52:49,210 --> 00:52:51,630
to do this.

995
00:52:51,630 --> 00:52:55,170
So it's like, kind of knew this was going to work.

996
00:52:55,170 --> 00:52:59,210
That's just saying we do some other things in the paper.

997
00:52:59,210 --> 00:53:01,530
We spent a whole lot of effort to try to build

998
00:53:01,530 --> 00:53:02,892
this theoretical framework.

999
00:53:02,892 --> 00:53:04,850
And the idea that we have now is actually maybe

1000
00:53:04,850 --> 00:53:08,770
it's going to be useful for other things.

1001
00:53:08,770 --> 00:53:11,970
And something I'm really excited about now

1002
00:53:11,970 --> 00:53:18,560
is this idea of duality and what we call modular duality.

1003
00:53:18,560 --> 00:53:20,020
So what does that mean?

1004
00:53:22,960 --> 00:53:25,980
And this is the thing which led to the big training speed-up.

1005
00:53:25,980 --> 00:53:30,600
So that's why we care about this, or one of the reasons.

1006
00:53:30,600 --> 00:53:35,600
So if you remember that we suppose now

1007
00:53:35,600 --> 00:53:37,800
that we came up with a norm such that

1008
00:53:37,800 --> 00:53:41,520
we could upper bound the error in the linearization

1009
00:53:41,520 --> 00:53:45,320
of our objective function.

1010
00:53:45,320 --> 00:53:47,880
And then we remember that because we did this

1011
00:53:47,880 --> 00:53:51,200
on the homework, that you can then derive steepest descent

1012
00:53:51,200 --> 00:53:53,280
optimization algorithms by solving

1013
00:53:53,280 --> 00:53:55,120
the argmin of this thing, finding

1014
00:53:55,120 --> 00:53:58,640
the minimizer of this thing.

1015
00:53:58,640 --> 00:54:01,240
And then you remember, because you did it on the homework,

1016
00:54:01,240 --> 00:54:05,040
that you can decouple the solutions for that problem

1017
00:54:05,040 --> 00:54:06,020
into two pieces.

1018
00:54:06,020 --> 00:54:09,160
One is evaluating a dual norm, and the other

1019
00:54:09,160 --> 00:54:12,940
is evaluating at argmax over a set of unit vectors.

1020
00:54:15,880 --> 00:54:17,380
And so we think about the purple box

1021
00:54:17,380 --> 00:54:19,080
as roughly being the step size.

1022
00:54:19,080 --> 00:54:24,340
And we can call this the second thing-- we can actually

1023
00:54:24,340 --> 00:54:27,500
call it a duality map.

1024
00:54:27,500 --> 00:54:28,920
And what does that mean?

1025
00:54:37,420 --> 00:54:40,380
Basically, there's this idea in more classical optimization

1026
00:54:40,380 --> 00:54:43,180
theory that the gradient is a dual vector

1027
00:54:43,180 --> 00:54:46,900
and the weights are a regular vector.

1028
00:54:46,900 --> 00:54:48,540
And the only thing that you're allowed

1029
00:54:48,540 --> 00:54:54,440
to do with dual vectors and regular vectors is combine them.

1030
00:54:54,440 --> 00:54:56,980
Well, basically, the dual vector is a linear map

1031
00:54:56,980 --> 00:55:00,020
that takes a regular vector and returns a real number.

1032
00:55:00,020 --> 00:55:01,860
And that's borne out here by saying

1033
00:55:01,860 --> 00:55:05,020
that if you have a gradient, the thing a gradient

1034
00:55:05,020 --> 00:55:08,100
is really, really what it's really good for is taking dot

1035
00:55:08,100 --> 00:55:11,580
products with weights and describing the linearization

1036
00:55:11,580 --> 00:55:12,700
of the function.

1037
00:55:12,700 --> 00:55:15,150
That's the only thing that you should ever really trust

1038
00:55:15,150 --> 00:55:17,690
the gradient will tell you is the linearization

1039
00:55:17,690 --> 00:55:18,710
of your function.

1040
00:55:18,710 --> 00:55:20,270
The idea that you can take a gradient

1041
00:55:20,270 --> 00:55:21,770
and just do gradient descent with it

1042
00:55:21,770 --> 00:55:25,450
is generically a bad idea and something

1043
00:55:25,450 --> 00:55:28,290
that we should all forget.

1044
00:55:28,290 --> 00:55:30,570
So that's like this idea of--

1045
00:55:30,570 --> 00:55:33,850
and it's the idea that a dual vector, even though it

1046
00:55:33,850 --> 00:55:36,357
may have the same dimensions, technically

1047
00:55:36,357 --> 00:55:38,690
you could add and subtract them because technically they

1048
00:55:38,690 --> 00:55:41,190
have the same dimensions, it can still be a bad idea.

1049
00:55:45,130 --> 00:55:49,110
What we need is a kind of formal procedure.

1050
00:55:49,110 --> 00:55:52,270
We need a procedure for converting dual vectors,

1051
00:55:52,270 --> 00:55:55,850
like the gradient, back to the primal space

1052
00:55:55,850 --> 00:55:59,790
such that we can add and subtract them.

1053
00:55:59,790 --> 00:56:02,230
But we should not just directly add and subtract them.

1054
00:56:02,230 --> 00:56:05,930
But this is an example of such a procedure.

1055
00:56:05,930 --> 00:56:09,593
And just looking at this argmax, what

1056
00:56:09,593 --> 00:56:11,010
you can think of it as saying is I

1057
00:56:11,010 --> 00:56:14,520
want to take a step of unit norm which

1058
00:56:14,520 --> 00:56:20,080
maximizes the linear improvement of my objective function.

1059
00:56:20,080 --> 00:56:23,960
And just subtracting the gradient from the weights

1060
00:56:23,960 --> 00:56:25,960
may not solve that problem.

1061
00:56:25,960 --> 00:56:27,900
AUDIENCE: Is that your beef with that

1062
00:56:27,900 --> 00:56:30,740
because we're doing nonlinear optimization, therefore,

1063
00:56:30,740 --> 00:56:33,880
you have no guarantee that your gradient plus the step size

1064
00:56:33,880 --> 00:56:35,242
results in an improvement?

1065
00:56:35,242 --> 00:56:37,700
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Well, for a sufficiently small step size,

1066
00:56:37,700 --> 00:56:39,620
it will give you an improvement.

1067
00:56:39,620 --> 00:56:43,840
It may just be really suboptimal because it may not solve--

1068
00:56:43,840 --> 00:56:47,158
there's a sense in which this thing is an optimal procedure

1069
00:56:47,158 --> 00:56:49,700
just in the sense that there is an optimization problem here.

1070
00:56:49,700 --> 00:56:50,860
There's a max.

1071
00:56:50,860 --> 00:56:52,360
There's an argmax.

1072
00:56:52,360 --> 00:56:55,320
So the solution the thing will solve some kind

1073
00:56:55,320 --> 00:56:57,060
of optimality criteria.

1074
00:56:57,060 --> 00:57:00,360
If you pick a good norm, it's like an optimality criterion

1075
00:57:00,360 --> 00:57:02,080
that you really care about.

1076
00:57:02,080 --> 00:57:06,560
And just moving in the gradient direction

1077
00:57:06,560 --> 00:57:09,140
is equivalent to saying that this is the Euclidean norm.

1078
00:57:09,140 --> 00:57:13,590
And so the optimization space in neural networks

1079
00:57:13,590 --> 00:57:17,830
is really unlikely to have a Euclidean structure.

1080
00:57:17,830 --> 00:57:19,090
So that's the argument.

1081
00:57:23,910 --> 00:57:26,550
And this is just a picture where it's

1082
00:57:26,550 --> 00:57:28,690
suggested by some other ideas in optimization.

1083
00:57:28,690 --> 00:57:32,188
But you think about the weight space--

1084
00:57:32,188 --> 00:57:33,730
the space that the gradient lives in,

1085
00:57:33,730 --> 00:57:36,250
you think of it as being a very distorted version.

1086
00:57:36,250 --> 00:57:38,550
So even though it may have the same dimensionality

1087
00:57:38,550 --> 00:57:40,550
as the weight space, the things that

1088
00:57:40,550 --> 00:57:43,990
live in the gradient space kind of maybe

1089
00:57:43,990 --> 00:57:45,610
are stretched and distorted.

1090
00:57:45,610 --> 00:57:50,042
So we need some procedure to undistort them

1091
00:57:50,042 --> 00:57:52,250
before we're going to subtract them from the weights.

1092
00:57:55,230 --> 00:57:57,790
So this is just to say that you should never do this

1093
00:57:57,790 --> 00:57:59,830
ever again unless you're really sure

1094
00:57:59,830 --> 00:58:01,370
that your space is Euclidean.

1095
00:58:04,190 --> 00:58:06,330
And that you should always think,

1096
00:58:06,330 --> 00:58:13,850
what is this dualization procedure that I really need

1097
00:58:13,850 --> 00:58:15,270
to think through what it is?

1098
00:58:18,090 --> 00:58:19,950
In a sense, this should not be surprising

1099
00:58:19,950 --> 00:58:22,740
because everyone tells you, if you're

1100
00:58:22,740 --> 00:58:24,490
going to do deep learning, you should just

1101
00:58:24,490 --> 00:58:25,670
use the Adam optimizer.

1102
00:58:25,670 --> 00:58:29,278
No one ever tells you to use regular gradient descent,

1103
00:58:29,278 --> 00:58:31,070
so there should be some idea that there is,

1104
00:58:31,070 --> 00:58:32,770
intuitively, we all know there's something

1105
00:58:32,770 --> 00:58:34,270
bad about regular gradient descent,

1106
00:58:34,270 --> 00:58:35,770
we just don't quite know what it is.

1107
00:58:39,610 --> 00:58:44,530
So this second thing is supposed to pass the type check.

1108
00:58:44,530 --> 00:58:48,290
And the question is, how do we come up

1109
00:58:48,290 --> 00:58:51,370
with such a dualization procedure for a neural net?

1110
00:58:51,370 --> 00:58:54,890
Well, if we built this, we had this recursive procedure

1111
00:58:54,890 --> 00:58:58,610
for inducing a norm on the weight space

1112
00:58:58,610 --> 00:58:59,750
of the full neural network.

1113
00:58:59,750 --> 00:59:01,970
So maybe there's a corresponding procedure

1114
00:59:01,970 --> 00:59:06,720
for recursively constructing a duality map.

1115
00:59:06,720 --> 00:59:08,640
So that's what we wanted to propose.

1116
00:59:08,640 --> 00:59:13,320
So it's the idea that, given individual layers, if you

1117
00:59:13,320 --> 00:59:15,720
have a norm on the weights of an individual layer,

1118
00:59:15,720 --> 00:59:19,560
you can solve a duality map for that layer.

1119
00:59:19,560 --> 00:59:21,620
And that's what you did on the homework

1120
00:59:21,620 --> 00:59:23,800
because we said that we're going to give

1121
00:59:23,800 --> 00:59:26,497
the linear layers a spectral norm,

1122
00:59:26,497 --> 00:59:29,080
and then we're going to solve the duality map that corresponds

1123
00:59:29,080 --> 00:59:30,300
to the spectral norm.

1124
00:59:33,160 --> 00:59:36,140
And then once we've solved those layer-wise duality maps,

1125
00:59:36,140 --> 00:59:39,360
it turns out that there is a valid recursive

1126
00:59:39,360 --> 00:59:41,280
procedure for combining them to come up

1127
00:59:41,280 --> 00:59:47,480
with a duality map on the full architecture, which I didn't

1128
00:59:47,480 --> 00:59:49,880
write it down in the slides, but maybe it's

1129
00:59:49,880 --> 00:59:51,340
something fun to think about.

1130
00:59:55,640 --> 00:59:58,960
AUDIENCE: So this would mean that the dualization of g

1131
00:59:58,960 --> 01:00:02,294
always has a norm of 1.

1132
01:00:02,294 --> 01:00:03,110
Oh, never mind.

1133
01:00:03,110 --> 01:00:08,230
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Yeah, it's because we broke things up

1134
01:00:08,230 --> 01:00:10,030
into two pieces.

1135
01:00:10,030 --> 01:00:12,272
AUDIENCE: Oh, so it's a projection in that space?

1136
01:00:12,272 --> 01:00:13,230
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: Sure.

1137
01:00:13,230 --> 01:00:17,990
So we broke up solving this argument.

1138
01:00:17,990 --> 01:00:21,990
We broke it up into solving an argmax over unit vectors

1139
01:00:21,990 --> 01:00:24,090
and then multiplying by the dual norm.

1140
01:00:26,630 --> 01:00:28,270
So there's also a recursive procedure

1141
01:00:28,270 --> 01:00:30,230
for computing the dual norm.

1142
01:00:30,230 --> 01:00:32,803
I just didn't talk about that on the slide.

1143
01:00:32,803 --> 01:00:34,470
AUDIENCE: So I guess I'm interpreting it

1144
01:00:34,470 --> 01:00:37,830
as we're finding whatever coordinate the projection

1145
01:00:37,830 --> 01:00:42,133
of the gradient maximizes--

1146
01:00:42,133 --> 01:00:43,550
I guess I'm just looking at argmax

1147
01:00:43,550 --> 01:00:45,450
of that in the next slide.

1148
01:00:45,450 --> 01:00:53,630
We're looking at the argmax of the inner product,

1149
01:00:53,630 --> 01:00:57,803
interpreting that as some of projection.

1150
01:00:57,803 --> 01:00:59,470
JEREMY BERNSTEIN: It doesn't necessarily

1151
01:00:59,470 --> 01:01:02,460
have to be a projection.

1152
01:01:02,460 --> 01:01:05,100
Actually, maybe it is, in some sense--

1153
01:01:05,100 --> 01:01:08,060
yes, maybe it is in the projection.

1154
01:01:08,060 --> 01:01:10,980
Definitely the one on the homework was a projection.

1155
01:01:10,980 --> 01:01:13,740
Yeah, I think, for norms, it probably usually

1156
01:01:13,740 --> 01:01:15,200
is a projection.

1157
01:01:19,140 --> 01:01:22,040
So yeah, I need to just think through that.

1158
01:01:22,040 --> 01:01:24,100
But I think you're probably right that you

1159
01:01:24,100 --> 01:01:25,980
can think of it as taking the gradient

1160
01:01:25,980 --> 01:01:27,840
and projecting it to the unit norm ball

1161
01:01:27,840 --> 01:01:29,760
but also distorting the direction.

1162
01:01:37,500 --> 01:01:44,220
But yeah, this is supposed to be you think about it as you've

1163
01:01:44,220 --> 01:01:47,220
got some Lipschitz guarantee and some Lipschitz smoothness

1164
01:01:47,220 --> 01:01:52,900
guarantee, which tell you how much the neural network

1165
01:01:52,900 --> 01:01:57,900
output is going to respond to changing its weights.

1166
01:01:57,900 --> 01:02:00,640
And so that, in particular, one of the things that

1167
01:02:00,640 --> 01:02:02,480
tells you is when the second-order changes

1168
01:02:02,480 --> 01:02:05,480
and the network output become important relative to the first

1169
01:02:05,480 --> 01:02:08,520
order changes, so a sensible thing to do

1170
01:02:08,520 --> 01:02:10,920
is to take an optimization step which

1171
01:02:10,920 --> 01:02:13,320
squeezes as much kind of juice out

1172
01:02:13,320 --> 01:02:17,120
of the linearization as possible while

1173
01:02:17,120 --> 01:02:19,080
making sure that the second-order network

1174
01:02:19,080 --> 01:02:21,250
effects don't dominate the first-order effects,

1175
01:02:21,250 --> 01:02:23,000
because the gradient really only tells you

1176
01:02:23,000 --> 01:02:24,580
about the first-order effects.

1177
01:02:24,580 --> 01:02:26,460
So that's an intuitive description.

1178
01:02:26,460 --> 01:02:29,860
And if you can solve that problem for individual layers,

1179
01:02:29,860 --> 01:02:33,540
how do you solve it for a full network?

1180
01:02:33,540 --> 01:02:36,000
And I'm just claiming that there's a recursive procedure

1181
01:02:36,000 --> 01:02:37,100
for doing that.

1182
01:02:40,360 --> 01:02:44,320
And this is just to say, to tell you a little backstory,

1183
01:02:44,320 --> 01:02:46,480
at least, why I became interested in this,

1184
01:02:46,480 --> 01:02:49,040
is because there's an optimization algorithm

1185
01:02:49,040 --> 01:02:51,240
that people at Google proposed called

1186
01:02:51,240 --> 01:02:57,040
Shampoo, which won this speed challenge at this industry

1187
01:02:57,040 --> 01:03:00,190
benchmarking competition.

1188
01:03:00,190 --> 01:03:05,432
But if you read the paper that proposes this method this,

1189
01:03:05,432 --> 01:03:07,390
first of all, the method that they actually use

1190
01:03:07,390 --> 01:03:10,110
is totally different to the method in the paper.

1191
01:03:10,110 --> 01:03:13,790
And it kind of feels like maybe there's not,

1192
01:03:13,790 --> 01:03:15,550
let's say, a complete understanding

1193
01:03:15,550 --> 01:03:18,190
of what the method is doing.

1194
01:03:18,190 --> 01:03:20,390
So I just wanted to look at this method

1195
01:03:20,390 --> 01:03:22,870
and strip away some of the things which people don't

1196
01:03:22,870 --> 01:03:23,690
actually use.

1197
01:03:23,690 --> 01:03:26,990
And it looks like the core primitive in this algorithm

1198
01:03:26,990 --> 01:03:30,070
is basically, for linear layers, it does this funny gradient

1199
01:03:30,070 --> 01:03:33,270
transformation.

1200
01:03:33,270 --> 01:03:36,510
And then realizing that this funny gradient transformation is

1201
01:03:36,510 --> 01:03:39,950
actually equivalent to that homework problem

1202
01:03:39,950 --> 01:03:43,970
that you all did, but when you read the paper,

1203
01:03:43,970 --> 01:03:45,330
they don't frame it that way.

1204
01:03:45,330 --> 01:03:47,430
So it's kind of interesting.

1205
01:03:47,430 --> 01:03:50,870
But it seems to really speed up the training.

1206
01:03:50,870 --> 01:03:53,830
So basically, what the claim is that hiding inside

1207
01:03:53,830 --> 01:03:56,220
of this optimization algorithm that people at Google

1208
01:03:56,220 --> 01:03:59,960
are really excited about is this very simple primitive.

1209
01:04:02,620 --> 01:04:06,560
And because we were working on this modular norm stuff,

1210
01:04:06,560 --> 01:04:08,660
it's very easy to implement this type of algorithm

1211
01:04:08,660 --> 01:04:10,900
because we just override the normalize function

1212
01:04:10,900 --> 01:04:17,540
of a linear module to do this spectral operation.

1213
01:04:17,540 --> 01:04:21,277
And then I implemented it in a simple notebook, just as

1214
01:04:21,277 --> 01:04:22,860
a proof of concept, and could actually

1215
01:04:22,860 --> 01:04:25,920
see that doing this special normalization,

1216
01:04:25,920 --> 01:04:28,952
it actually leads to training much faster than the thing

1217
01:04:28,952 --> 01:04:30,660
in the middle, which is just like a naive

1218
01:04:30,660 --> 01:04:33,380
form of spectral normalization.

1219
01:04:33,380 --> 01:04:36,700
And both of them are much better than just vanilla gradient

1220
01:04:36,700 --> 01:04:37,360
descent.

1221
01:04:39,940 --> 01:04:44,460
And then there's this open-source neural network

1222
01:04:44,460 --> 01:04:46,220
speedrunning community.

1223
01:04:46,220 --> 01:04:51,340
And this person, Keller Jordan, made a really, really fast

1224
01:04:51,340 --> 01:04:56,070
implementation of this spectral dualization procedure.

1225
01:04:56,070 --> 01:04:57,690
It turns out, at the moment, it's

1226
01:04:57,690 --> 01:05:01,830
the fastest way to train nanoGPT on this speedrun.

1227
01:05:01,830 --> 01:05:04,730
So it's seems that's actually--

1228
01:05:04,730 --> 01:05:07,090
the worry with the technique is maybe, in principle,

1229
01:05:07,090 --> 01:05:08,465
it's the right thing to be doing.

1230
01:05:08,465 --> 01:05:13,410
But it's so expensive to do these spectral normalization

1231
01:05:13,410 --> 01:05:15,355
operations that it's practically useless.

1232
01:05:15,355 --> 01:05:16,730
But it turns out there's actually

1233
01:05:16,730 --> 01:05:20,050
a way to implement this dualization procedure really

1234
01:05:20,050 --> 01:05:24,410
fast using an iterative method.

1235
01:05:24,410 --> 01:05:27,732
So this is to say that what I told you--

1236
01:05:27,732 --> 01:05:29,190
when we wrote this modular package,

1237
01:05:29,190 --> 01:05:32,730
we actually kind of did it wrong because when

1238
01:05:32,730 --> 01:05:35,530
we said that we should normalize,

1239
01:05:35,530 --> 01:05:37,270
that is actually not optimal.

1240
01:05:37,270 --> 01:05:40,290
We should actually do this dualization procedure.

1241
01:05:40,290 --> 01:05:44,082
And it should recursively compute the dualization

1242
01:05:44,082 --> 01:05:45,790
based on the architecture of the network.

1243
01:05:45,790 --> 01:05:48,750
So we still need to update the package to do that.

1244
01:05:52,270 --> 01:05:57,863
So in conclusion, yeah, I really like this idea

1245
01:05:57,863 --> 01:06:00,030
that we should think about building a neural network

1246
01:06:00,030 --> 01:06:02,750
as building LEGO, and that you should

1247
01:06:02,750 --> 01:06:04,210
be able to build whatever you want,

1248
01:06:04,210 --> 01:06:07,230
and you should be able to have a theoretical understanding

1249
01:06:07,230 --> 01:06:10,550
of what you build, and it should be

1250
01:06:10,550 --> 01:06:16,030
based on thinking about just how you combine individual bricks.

1251
01:06:16,030 --> 01:06:18,890
And so far, we've been thinking about these scaling issues

1252
01:06:18,890 --> 01:06:23,210
and now, apparently, also these training speed records.

1253
01:06:26,230 --> 01:06:29,270
And I think that this idea has not really

1254
01:06:29,270 --> 01:06:31,650
proliferated at all really.

1255
01:06:31,650 --> 01:06:36,230
But I think it can be a more generally useful idea.

1256
01:06:36,230 --> 01:06:41,277
So if you want to save energy and money and time,

1257
01:06:41,277 --> 01:06:43,110
a good way to do that is to make your models

1258
01:06:43,110 --> 01:06:44,590
really low precision.

1259
01:06:44,590 --> 01:06:48,630
there's no one has a theoretical understanding of how

1260
01:06:48,630 --> 01:06:50,840
precision in a neural network should behave.

1261
01:06:50,840 --> 01:06:58,300
So as far as I can tell, all the research is just trying stuff.

1262
01:06:58,300 --> 01:07:00,580
But intuitively, precision and continuity

1263
01:07:00,580 --> 01:07:02,260
are very closely, very intimately

1264
01:07:02,260 --> 01:07:06,322
related concepts because if you want

1265
01:07:06,322 --> 01:07:07,780
to quantize as much as you can, you

1266
01:07:07,780 --> 01:07:11,220
need to know what discretization scale the space

1267
01:07:11,220 --> 01:07:15,140
is kind of continuous on or something like that.

1268
01:07:15,140 --> 01:07:18,232
And similarly, these classic adversarial robustness things,

1269
01:07:18,232 --> 01:07:19,940
I think, should also be related to having

1270
01:07:19,940 --> 01:07:22,300
a good understanding of the continuity

1271
01:07:22,300 --> 01:07:24,940
properties of the space.

1272
01:07:24,940 --> 01:07:29,860
So you want to cast these as questions of module sensitivity.

1273
01:07:29,860 --> 01:07:32,920
And I want to turn this into an open-source project.

1274
01:07:32,920 --> 01:07:35,500
So I made this website, but it doesn't have very much

1275
01:07:35,500 --> 01:07:36,640
information there yet.

1276
01:07:36,640 --> 01:07:39,240
But anyway, that's it.

1277
01:07:39,240 --> 01:07:41,430
So yeah, thanks.
